/* Chapter 3 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":3,"title":"The Main Prohibitions and Business Ethics in Islamic Economics and Finance","part":"part-i","partRoman":"I","pages":[43,72],"minutes":75,"estimates":{"f1":75,"f2":90},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch3-1","text":"State the Qur’anic and Sunnah basis of the prohibition of Riba.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-2","text":"Define Riba, Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl and apply the exchange rules of Bai‘ al Sarf.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-3","text":"Distinguish sale, loan and lease by their effect on ownership, risk and liability.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-4","text":"Evaluate common arguments used to justify interest and the author’s responses.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-5","text":"Define Gharar and Jahl, distinguish excessive from minor Gharar, and list its main forms.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-6","text":"Define Maisir and Qimar and apply the tests for permissible and prohibited lotteries/prize schemes.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-7","text":"Explain the Islamic business norms: justice, honesty, Najash, Khalabah, disclosure, covenants, cooperation, fair pricing and Dharar.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-8","text":"State the Qur'anic and Hadith basis for the prohibition of Riba and trace the chronology of its revelation","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-9","text":"Distinguish Riba al-Nasiah (Riba in loans/debts) from Riba al-Fadl (Riba in sale/exchange transactions), with examples","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-10","text":"Explain why a Qard (loan) must be repaid at exactly the principal, and how Bai' (sale), loan and Ijarah (lease) differ in ownership/risk transfer","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-11","text":"Rebut the common misconceptions used to argue that modern commercial interest is not Riba","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-12","text":"Explain the author's stated rationale (distributive justice) for the prohibition of interest, and how it differs from conventional treatment of capital","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-13","text":"Define Gharar, distinguish excessive (Kathir) from minor (Qalil) uncertainty, and list categories/examples of Gharar-vitiated sales","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-14","text":"Define Maisir/Qimar and identify which financial schemes (lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, price-difference-settled futures/options) fall under this prohibition","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch3-15","text":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"These prohibitions define the outer boundary of every Islamic finance contract. Whether a product is Murabaha, Ijarah, Sukuk or Takaful, the first test is always: does it involve Riba, excessive Gharar or gambling? The business norms in this chapter also explain later rules on penalties, promises, disclosure and Takaful.","whyExpanded":"This chapter defines the non-negotiable Shari'ah boundaries — the prohibitions of Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar — that every Islamic financial product and contract in the rest of the book must be tested against, plus the positive ethical norms (honesty, disclosure, fair dealing) that govern how Islamic finance is meant to be practiced. It is the conceptual core the author says is 'least controversial' yet most heavily emphasized in the book.","overview":"The chapter sets out the three basic prohibitions — Riba, Gharar and Maisir/Qimar — which demarcate invalid contracts, followed by the business ethics and norms of Shari’ah. The discussion of Riba is the most detailed: its textual basis, its application to loans, debts and exchanges, common misconceptions, its connotation and rationale.","summary":"Riba, Gharar and games of chance are the main prohibitions. Any increase over the principal of a loan or debt against nothing but time is Riba, and Shari’ah prohibits all benefits accruing without labour, risk or expertise; indexation of loans is forbidden (OIC Fiqh Academy), though one may lend in terms of gold or another currency without the liability increasing. Gharar is excessive uncertainty about the subject matter, price or obligations; to be prohibited it must be major and affect principal aspects, and it can be avoided by meeting standards of certainty (as in Salam). Many conventional derivatives involve major Gharar, and gambling (a form of Gharar) covers lotteries and draw-based prize schemes. Business must follow ethical standards: honesty, fair dealing, free consent, disclosure and no misrepresentation.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 3 establishes the non-negotiable Shari'ah boundaries for Islamic finance: the prohibitions of Riba (interest, in both its loan-based Nasiah form and its exchange-based Fadl form), Gharar (excessive contractual uncertainty), and Maisir/Qimar (gambling). It traces Riba's Qur'anic and Hadith basis in detail, systematically rebuts common arguments for exempting modern commercial interest, and grounds the prohibition's rationale in distributive justice — money-capital must share risk to earn any return, unlike land (rent) or labour (wages). It defines Gharar's excessive/minor distinction and catalogs its categories, and explains Maisir/Qimar's application to lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance and speculative derivatives. The chapter closes with the positive ethical framework — justice, honesty, prohibitions on Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-keeping, mutual cooperation (the root of Takaful), fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar — that together define how Islamic business is meant to be conducted, not merely what it must avoid.","summarySection":"3.4","takeaways":["Creditor is entitled to principal only: any excess on a loan/debt is Riba, whatever the rate or purpose.","Riba Al-Nasiah (delay; loans) and Riba Al-Fadl (excess in same-genus exchange).","Currencies: excess allowed, delay not allowed.","Sale transfers ownership permanently and may include profit; a credit price cannot increase after execution.","Only excessive Gharar is prohibited; Salam and Istisna‘a meet standards of certainty.","Prize bonds and conventional insurance involve Riba and Maisir.","Najash, Khalabah, Ghaban and concealment are prohibited; promises are binding with recovery of actual loss.","There is unanimous Muslim agreement that Riba is prohibited; disagreement concerns only its scope — the chapter argues modern commercial/bank interest unambiguously falls within it.","The core Riba rule: a creditor (in loans or debts) may recover only the principal (Ra'asul-mal); any addition, however small, is Riba — with the profit-margin-vs-Riba distinction turning on timing (embedded in the original sale price vs added after a debt is fixed).","Riba al-Nasiah (delay-based, loans/debts) and Riba al-Fadl (quality/quantity-based, same-genus exchange transactions, per the six-commodities Hadith) are the two forms of Riba; the comprehensive definition is any increase over principal not matched by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise.","The author rebuts seven common arguments for exempting modern interest from the Riba prohibition, and identifies distributive justice as the strongest rationale for the prohibition, citing extensive economic evidence (Kester 1986, Mohsin Khan 1986) against interest's claimed benefits.","Money-capital cannot earn a guaranteed return in Islamic finance; it earns profit only by bearing risk of loss, unlike land (rent) and labour (wages) — this is the foundational Islam-vs-capitalism distinction in factor payments.","Gharar (excessive uncertainty) is prohibited only in its major/excessive form (Gharar-e-Kathir); minor, unavoidable uncertainty (Gharar Qalil) is tolerated, since risk-bearing is itself required for legitimate profit.","Maisir/Qimar (gambling) covers lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, and price-difference-settled futures/options; a valid lot-drawing requires no pre-existing vested right and no deprivation of what participants already possess.","Positive business ethics — justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash/Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-keeping, mutual cooperation ('Aqilah/Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq, the roots of Takaful), fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar — complete the Shari'ah framework governing how Islamic business must actually be conducted."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you cite the key verses (2:275–281, 3:130) and state the principal-only rule?","Can you distinguish sale, loan and lease by ownership and risk?","Can you rebut three misconceptions about interest?","Can you apply Nawavi’s three exchange rules?","Can you give the broad definition of Riba and what it excludes?","Can you define Gharar, classify its forms and explain Gharar-e-Kathir vs Qalil?","Can you distinguish permissible drawing of lots from prohibited lotteries?","Can you define Najash, Khalabah, Ghaban-e-Fahish and Dharar?"],"topics":[{"id":"t3.1","section":"3.1","title":"Introduction: Prohibitions, Norms and Ethics","pages":[43,43],"tier":"revision","concepts":["riba","gharar","maisir"],"intuition":"Freedom of contract in Islam has boundaries. What are they?","simpleExplanation":"Islam allows business freely but within limits: the major prohibitions (Riba, Maisir/Qimar, Gharar) and norms of ethics that prevent injustice and harm.","academicExplanation":["Islam constrains freedom in business and finance through prohibitions, ethics and norms to avoid inequitable gains and injustice. Shari’ah compliance is the raison d’être of the Islamic financial system and should dominate all other concerns of IFIs; only through compliance can the system develop sustainably and ensure fairness. Invalid contracts arising from contractual deficiencies are discussed in Chapter 5."],"examExplanation":"Shari’ah compliance is the raison d’être of IFIs. Boundaries: Riba, Maisir/Qimar, Gharar + business ethics and norms.","keyPoints":["Shari’ah compliance should dominate all other concerns of IFIs.","Shari’ah compliance is the raison d’être of Islamic finance; it must dominate every other concern of an IFI.","Invalid contracts (contractual defects) are treated separately in Chapter 5."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why compliance comes first","kind":"practice","text":"An Islamic bank finds a product that would double its profit but uses a buy-back. Because Shari’ah compliance is the raison d’être of the institution, the profit consideration cannot override it (p. 43)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.2","t3.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the author call the raison d’être of the Islamic financial system?","options":["Profitability","Shari’ah compliance","Low cost of funds","Government support"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Shari’ah compliance (p. 43)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t3.2","section":"3.2","title":"The Basic Prohibitions","pages":[43,43],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","gharar","maisir"],"intuition":"Which prohibitions matter most when judging whether a transaction is valid?","simpleExplanation":"Islamic law does not recognise transactions with an illegitimate factor or object. The prohibition of Riba, Gharar and gambling is the most strategic factor defining invalid and voidable contracts.","academicExplanation":["As a rule, Islamic law does not recognise transactions with a proven illegitimate factor and/or object. The ban on Riba, Gharar and gambling is the single most important test for telling valid contracts from void or voidable ones — it marks the boundary a transaction must not cross."],"examExplanation":"Riba, Gharar and gambling = most strategic prohibitions; they define invalid/voidable contracts.","keyPoints":["Three basic prohibitions: Riba, Gharar, Maisir/Qimar.","Riba, Gharar and gambling are the strategic factors that define invalid and voidable contracts."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Screening three deals","kind":"practice","text":"(1) A loan of 100 repayable 110 — Riba. (2) Selling next year’s unknown fish catch for a fixed price — Gharar. (3) A prize draw funded by participants’ stakes — Maisir. Each fails a different basic prohibition (p. 43)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1","t3.2.2","t3.2.3","t5.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which three prohibitions define the overall limits for transactions?","options":["Riba, Gharar, gambling","Zakat, Waqf, Hibah","Murabaha, Salam, Ijarah","Taxes, rent, wages"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"Riba, Gharar and gambling (p. 43)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"table":{"caption":"The three basic prohibitions at a glance","head":["Prohibition","Core idea","Tolerance"],"rows":[["Riba","Any addition over the principal of a loan/debt; unequal or deferred exchange of like monetary goods","None — the slightest Riba vitiates"],["Gharar","Excessive uncertainty about the subject matter, price or delivery","Minor (Qalil) Gharar tolerated"],["Maisir / Qimar","Gain by chance at another’s cost","None"]]}},{"id":"t3.2.1","section":"3.2.1","title":"Riba: Textual Prohibition and Riba in Loans/Debts","pages":[44,49],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","riba-nasiah","qard","dayn"],"intuition":"Everyone agrees Riba is prohibited — the disagreement is about what counts as Riba. Start with the texts and the core rule: the creditor is entitled to the principal only.","simpleExplanation":"The Qur’an prohibits Riba in several verses, finally in 2:275–281: “God has permitted trade and forbidden Riba … yours is your principal.” So on any loan or debt, anything above the principal — however small — is Riba. Conventional bank interest on loans is therefore Riba.","academicExplanation":["There is no difference of opinion among Muslims about the prohibition of Riba, which all sects consider a severe sin; the differences concern its meaning. Myths persist: some liberals consider commercial interest not to be Riba; some pious Muslims think any prefixed return is Riba; some businesses expect costless money from Islamic banks; some economists think the profit margin on credit sales resembles Riba.","Qur’anic verses in order of revelation: 30:39; 4:161; 3:130 (first express prohibition for Muslims, around the battle of Uhad, 3 AH); 2:275–281 (final prohibition in 10 AH). The background of 2:278–279 (Banu Thaqif’s claim to interest after the treaty) is narrated from Justice Taqi Usmani’s judgement. Traditions: the Prophet (pbuh) cursed the receiver, payer, recorder and witnesses of interest; a lender should not accept even a dish or a ride from the borrower unless customary between them; pre-Islamic Riba increased the debt when the period expired; at the last Hajj every form of Riba was cancelled, starting with that owed to Abbas; the six-commodities Hadith; and the dates Hadiths (Bilal and Khyber).","A loan (Qard) is anything taken with liability to return the same or similar when demanded; a debt (Dayn) is a liability arising from a credit transaction such as a credit sale or due rentals in Ijarah, payable at a stipulated time. The Qur’anic principle (2:278–279) is that in both, the creditor is entitled only to Ra’as al-mal (principal) — exactly the loan amount, or the debt generated by the credit transaction. Any amount, big or small, over and above the principal is Riba. Since conventional bank financing is a loan carrying a premium, commercial interest is Riba.","Riba, meaning prohibited gain, is juxtaposed in the Qur’an against profit from sale: all earnings are either profit from trade and business along with its liability (permitted), or return on cash or a converted form of cash without bearing liability for the result of deployed capital (prohibited)."],"examExplanation":"Qur’an 2:275–281: trade permitted, Riba forbidden; creditor gets principal (Ra’as al-mal) only. Any excess over principal of a loan (Qard) or debt (Dayn) is Riba, whatever the size. Earnings = profit with liability (permitted) or return on cash without liability (prohibited). Commercial interest = Riba.","keyPoints":["No disagreement on the prohibition itself — only on what constitutes Riba.","Verse 3:130 was the first express prohibition for Muslims (around Uhad, 3 AH); final prohibition 10 AH.","All parties — receiver, payer, scribe, witnesses — share the guilt.","Even a small benefit (a dish, a ride) from a borrower is to be refused unless customary.","Creditor is entitled to the principal only in loans and in debts.","Limited permission may exist for a destitute compelled to borrow for basic food needs; not for luxury or business expansion.","Express prohibition came around the battle of Uhud (3 AH); the final, repeated prohibition in 10 AH.","A destitute person compelled to borrow for basic food may have limited permission; borrowing on interest for luxury or business expansion is culpable."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba","definition":"Literally excess; in Shari’ah an addition, however slight, over and above the principal of a loan or debt."},{"term":"Qard (loan)","definition":"Any commodity or money taken with liability to return the same or similar when demanded by the creditor."},{"term":"Dayn (debt)","definition":"A liability to pay arising from a credit transaction (e.g. credit sale, due rentals), payable at a stipulated time."},{"term":"Ra’as al-mal","definition":"The principal amount of a loan or debt — all the creditor is entitled to."}],"examples":[{"title":"Banu Thaqif and verses 2:278–279","kind":"textbook","text":"After embracing Islam, Banu Thaqif claimed outstanding interest from Banu Amr under a proposed treaty. The matter reached the Prophet (pbuh) through the Governor of Makkah, and verses 278–279 were revealed: give up the remaining Riba — “yours is your principal”. Banu Thaqif surrendered the claim (p. 45)."},{"title":"Bilal’s dates","kind":"textbook","text":"Bilal exchanged two measures of lower-quality dates for one of higher quality. The Prophet (pbuh) called it Riba and told him to sell the first dates for money and buy the better dates with the proceeds (p. 46)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Only the lender commits a sin in an interest transaction.","right":"The Hadith of Jabir: the receiver, payer, recorder and witnesses are all alike in guilt."},{"wrong":"Any predetermined profit in any transaction is Riba.","right":"The author calls this a myth: Riba concerns the excess over the principal of a loan/debt; profit in a valid sale is permitted."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t7.2","t7.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to verses 2:278–279, what is a creditor entitled to?","options":["Principal plus a fair rate","Principal only","Principal indexed to inflation","Whatever the debtor agrees"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"“If you repent, yours is your principal” — any excess over principal is Riba (p. 47)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"Prohibition of Riba — Qur'an and Sunnah","title":"Prohibition of Riba: Qur'anic Revelation Chronology and Hadith Evidence","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author establishes that all Muslim sects unanimously prohibit Riba, traces the chronological revelation of the Qur'anic verses prohibiting it, and presents key Hadith establishing its severity and specific forms.","simpleExplanation":"Every school of Islamic thought agrees interest is forbidden — the disagreement is only about exactly what counts as Riba. The Qur'an's prohibition came gradually (starting with mild disapproval, ending with an absolute, final ban shortly before the Prophet's death), and multiple sayings of the Prophet reinforce it: interest is cursed, even accepting a small gift from a borrower can be Riba, and famously, at his final pilgrimage the Prophet cancelled all outstanding Riba, starting with his own uncle's.","academicExplanation":"The author stresses there is no disagreement among Muslims about Riba's prohibition (grounded in explicit Qur'an/Sunnah condemnation); disagreement concerns only what constitutes Riba. He identifies common myths (e.g. that commercial interest isn't Riba, or that Islamic banks should offer costless money, or that credit-sale profit margins resemble Riba) that need correcting among Shari'ah scholars, academicians and bankers. The Qur'anic prohibition unfolded in stages: initial disapproval in the Makkah period, an express prohibition before the Battle of Uhud (3 AH), and the final, repeated prohibition in 10 AH, about two weeks before the Prophet's death. Key verses in order of revelation: Al-Rum 30:39 (Riba given to increase wealth does not increase with God, unlike charity); Al-Nisa 4:161 (condemns the Jews' taking of Riba as wrongful appropriation); Al-e-Imran 3:130 (prohibits 'doubled and redoubled' Riba — per Justice Taqi Usmani, the first verse expressly forbidding Riba for Muslims, around the time of Uhud); and Al-Baqarah 2:275-281, the most comprehensive passage — declaring those who take Riba are like those maddened by Satan's touch, refuting the claim 'trade is just like Riba' (Allah permitted trade, forbade Riba), commanding believers to give up outstanding Riba on pain of 'war from Allah and His Messenger', but allowing recovery of the principal, and instructing patience/charity toward a debtor in genuine hardship. Justice Taqi Usmani's account of the revelation context of verses 278-279 (the Banu Thaqif dispute over outstanding interest after their post-conquest treaty with the Prophet) is given as a concrete case showing the prohibition applied even to previously agreed interest claims. Key Hadith: the Prophet cursed the receiver, payer, recorder and witnesses of Riba equally (Jabir); a lender must not accept even a gift/ride from a borrower unless such exchanges were already mutual custom (Anas ibn Malik) — establishing that even non-monetary incidental benefit to a lender can be Riba; a description of pagan-Arabia Riba practice, where a creditor at maturity would ask for full payment or an increased amount with extended time (Zaid b. Aslam) — foreshadowing the roll-over/compounding critique; and the Prophet's Farewell Hajj declaration that 'every form of Riba is cancelled', starting by cancelling the Riba owed to his own uncle Abbas, establishing finality and impartiality. Two further Hadith (the 'six commodities' Hadith and the dates-for-dates exchange incidents) extend the prohibition to unequal exchanges of the same commodity type — laying the groundwork for Riba al-Fadl (covered in the next topic).","examExplanation":"All Muslims agree Riba is prohibited (per explicit Qur'an/Sunnah text); the only dispute is over its scope. Revelation chronology: Al-Rum 30:39 (earliest, mild) → Al-Nisa 4:161 → Al-e-Imran 3:130 (first express prohibition for Muslims, doubled/redoubled Riba, ~Battle of Uhud) → Al-Baqarah 2:275-281 (final, comprehensive: Riba-takers likened to the Satan-maddened; 'Allah has permitted trade and forbidden Riba'; command to forgo outstanding Riba on pain of 'war from Allah and His Messenger'; principal only may be recovered; patience urged for a hardship-stricken debtor). Key Hadith: curse on all four parties to a Riba transaction (lender, borrower, recorder, witnesses — 'they are all alike in guilt'); prohibition on a lender accepting even a non-monetary gift/favour from a borrower beyond prior mutual custom; the Farewell Hajj cancellation of all Riba, beginning with the Prophet's own uncle's.","keyPoints":["There is no disagreement among Muslims that Riba is prohibited; disagreement exists only over what constitutes Riba — a distinction the author says must be clarified among scholars, academics and bankers to remove common myths.","The Qur'anic prohibition of Riba unfolded in stages: mild disapproval (Makkah period) → express prohibition (~3 AH, before Uhud, via Al-e-Imran 3:130) → final, repeated, comprehensive prohibition (10 AH, weeks before the Prophet's death, via Al-Baqarah 2:275-281).","Al-Baqarah 2:275 directly refutes the claim 'trade is just like Riba': 'Allah has permitted trade and forbidden Riba.'","Al-Baqarah 2:279 establishes that a repentant creditor may recover only the principal ('Ra'asul-mal'), nothing more — the foundational textual basis for defining Riba as any addition over principal.","A Hadith (Jabir) curses the receiver, payer, recorder and witnesses of Riba equally — 'they are all alike [in guilt]' — implicating all facilitators of a Riba transaction, not just the direct lender/borrower.","A lender may not accept even an incidental non-monetary benefit (e.g. a gift or a ride) from a borrower unless such exchanges were already customary between them prior to the loan — showing Riba can take non-cash forms.","At his Farewell Hajj, the Prophet declared all Riba cancelled and began by cancelling the amounts owed to his own uncle Abbas, establishing the prohibition's universality and impartiality."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba","definition":"Literally an excess or increase; in Shari'ah terminology, an addition, however slight, over and above the principal of a loan or debt."},{"term":"Ra'asul-mal","definition":"The principal amount of a loan, a debt, or money invested in a business — per Qur'an 2:279, this is all a creditor may recover from a defaulting or repentant debtor."}],"conditions":["A creditor is entitled to recover the principal (Ra'asul-mal) only — any addition over the principal, however small, constitutes Riba."],"principles":["'Allah has permitted trade and forbidden Riba' (Qur'an 2:275) — the foundational textual distinction between legitimate trade profit and prohibited Riba."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Banu Thaqif dispute (revelation context of Qur'an 2:278-279)","kind":"textbook","text":"After the conquest of Makkah, the Prophet voided all outstanding Riba amounts. The Banu Thaqif tribe, in a post-conversion treaty, tried to retain their right to collect interest owed to them while their own creditors would forgo interest owed by them. The Prophet rejected this asymmetry, ruling Banu Thaqif would have the same rights as other Muslims. When Banu Thaqif still tried to claim interest from their debtors (Banu Amr Ibn-al-Mughirah), the matter went to the Governor of Makkah and then the Prophet, prompting revelation of verses 2:278-279 commanding believers to give up all outstanding Riba."},{"title":"The Farewell Hajj cancellation","kind":"textbook","text":"At his last Hajj, the Prophet declared: 'Every form of Riba is cancelled; capital indeed is yours which you shall have; wrong not and you shall not be wronged... I start with the amount of Riba which people owe to my uncle Abbas and declare it all cancelled.'"}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the Riba prohibition was a single, one-time revelation; in fact it unfolded gradually across roughly seven years, culminating in the comprehensive Al-Baqarah verses shortly before the Prophet's death."],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Riba al-Nasiah","Riba al-Fadl","Qard","Dayn"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,45,46,47]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Qard (loan)","b":"Dayn (debt)","text":"Qard is money or a fungible taken with the obligation to return its like on demand; Dayn is a liability from a credit transaction (credit sale, due rent) payable at an agreed time. In both, the creditor is entitled to the principal only (p. 47)."}],"principles":["In loans and debts, the creditor is entitled to the principal only; any excess is Riba.","Profit with liability is permitted; return on cash without liability is prohibited."],"table":{"caption":"Qur’anic verses on Riba in order of revelation (as listed)","head":["Verse","Content (paraphrased)"],"rows":[["30:39","Riba does not increase with God; charity multiplies"],["4:161","Taking Riba, though forbidden, condemned"],["3:130","Do not take doubled and redoubled Riba — first express prohibition for Muslims"],["2:275–281","Trade permitted, Riba forbidden; give up remaining Riba; war from Allah and His Messenger; your principal is yours; respite for the debtor in difficulty"]]}},{"id":"t3.2.1.b","section":"3.2.1","title":"Riba: Distinguishing Sale, Loan and Lease","pages":[48,49],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","bai","ijarah","qard"],"intuition":"A credit sale price is higher than the cash price, and interest makes a loan cost more. Why is one allowed and the other not? The answer lies in what each contract does to ownership, risk and liability.","simpleExplanation":"In a sale, ownership passes permanently to the buyer, and the seller may include a profit even on credit. In a loan, the same asset must be returned — so no extra can be charged. In a lease, ownership stays with the lessor who earns rent and bears ownership risk; money cannot be leased because it is consumed in use.","academicExplanation":["All real-sector transactions involve (1) sale/purchase, cash or credit; (2) loaning; (3) leasing — each with different implications for ownership, risk and liability. In Bai‘ (sale), ownership transfers definitely and permanently at execution, whether payment is spot or deferred, and the price may include a profit margin. In Salam, both parties are obliged to give/take delivery at the agreed time on agreed terms whatever the price then. In Hibah (gift), ownership transfers permanently free of payment.","A loan, always free of charge, is a temporary transfer of ownership: the debtor must return the same asset. Riba is also a temporary transfer but with payment of interest. In Ijarah, ownership does not transfer; only the usufruct is provided for rent, and the lessor bears ownership-related expenses and loss. Anything consumed or changed in form in use (yarn, money, edibles, fuel) cannot be leased — hence rent on houses and vehicles is permissible, while rent on money is prohibited.","Verse 2:275 records the objection “sale is just like Riba”: a price can be increased for deferred payment in the original sale, yet increasing the amount after maturity is Riba. The Qur’an’s reply — Allah permitted trade and forbade Riba — means parties may bargain while the price is not stipulated; once the sale is executed and the price fixed, it becomes a debt payable without any increase. Riba is wider than interest: interest (a charge for use of money) is always Riba, but Riba also applies to non-monetary exchanges."],"examExplanation":"Sale: permanent ownership transfer, profit allowed (cash or credit). Loan: temporary transfer, return same — no charge. Lease: usufruct only, lessor bears ownership risk, rent allowed; money cannot be leased. Credit price may be bargained before execution; after execution the price is a debt that cannot increase.","keyPoints":["Determine whether a transaction is a sale or a loan, and when a credit sale is executed.","Once the credit price is fixed and the sale executed, no addition can be charged.","Money cannot be leased since it is consumed in use.","Riba is a wider term than interest.","“Interest” (a charge for the use of money) is always Riba, but Riba is wider — it also covers non-monetary exchanges such as foreign exchange."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Yarn cannot be leased","kind":"textbook","text":"Yarn, when used, becomes cloth; it can be bought and sold but not leased — just as money cannot be rented out."},{"title":"Why yarn and money cannot be leased","kind":"textbook","text":"Things consumed or transformed in use — yarn, money, edibles, fuel — can be sold but not leased; houses and vehicles can be leased because their corpus remains (p. 48)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Charging more for a credit sale than a cash sale is Riba.","right":"The price may be bargained keeping in mind the credit period; the prohibition applies to increasing the debt after the sale is executed."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.3.1","t6.5.3","t11.2.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why can a house be leased but money cannot?","options":["Houses are more valuable","Money is consumed/changes in use; a house’s corpus remains","Money is haram","Leasing is prohibited generally"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Anything consumed in use or changing shape cannot be leased (p. 48)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"Riba in Loans/Debts — How to Distinguish Transactions","title":"Riba in Loans and Debts: Distinguishing Sale, Loan and Lease","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author explains the Qard (loan) vs Dayn (debt) distinction, states the core rule that only the principal may be recovered, and shows how ownership/risk transfer differs across sale (Bai'), loan (Qard) and lease (Ijarah) — the key to telling legitimate profit from Riba.","simpleExplanation":"A loan (Qard) is money/goods you must return exactly as given — no more, no less; charging anything extra on a loan is Riba. A debt (Dayn) arises from a credit sale or unpaid rent — again, the seller can build a profit margin into the original agreed price, but can't add anything once that price is fixed and becomes a debt. Selling transfers ownership permanently; a loan transfers it only temporarily and must be returned identically; leasing never transfers ownership at all — only the right to use something, which is why you can lease a house but never lease out money (money is 'used up' the moment you spend it, so there's nothing to return in its original form to collect rent on).","academicExplanation":"The chapter's core practical question: how to distinguish which gains are legitimate profit versus prohibited Riba. Since conventional-bank financing is legally structured as a loan carrying a premium, the author states unequivocally this is Riba under the Qur'anic principle. A Qard (loan) is any commodity/money taken with a liability to return the same or similar commodity/amount when demanded; a Dayn (debt) is a liability to pay arising from a credit transaction (e.g. sale on credit, or accrued Ijarah rentals) — payable at the stipulated time, with the creditor unable to demand earlier payment. Per Qur'an 2:278-279, in both loans and debts the creditor's right is limited to the Ra'asul-mal (principal) — any addition, big or small, is Riba. The chapter frames all real-sector business transactions as falling into three types — sale/purchase (cash or credit), loaning, and leasing — each with different ownership/risk implications: in Bai' (sale), ownership transfers definitively and permanently at execution regardless of whether payment is spot or deferred (deferred payment may legitimately embed a profit margin as part of the agreed price); in Salam (a special forward sale), both parties are bound to transfer ownership at a specified future time per agreed terms, irrespective of later price moves; a gift (Hibah) transfers ownership permanently and immediately, free of charge. A loan is always free of charge in Islamic finance — it transfers temporary ownership of goods/assets with an obligation to return the same asset, with no payment permitted (charging on a loan is Riba). Ijarah is categorically different: ownership of the leased asset never transfers, only its usufruct, against rent — the lessor retains ownership and thus bears ownership-related expenses/risks (asset loss); critically, only assets whose corpus is not consumed/altered by use can be leased (houses, vehicles) — items like yarn, money, edibles or fuel, which are consumed or transformed by use, cannot be leased, which is precisely why 'rent' on money (interest) is prohibited while rent on a house is not. The author explains the classic 'trade is like Riba' objection (Qur'an 2:275): usurers argued that a seller may raise a price for deferred payment (a valid sale), so charging an add-on to an overdue debt should be equally valid since 'the increase in both cases is similar.' The Qur'an's answer is definitional, not just moral: once a sale is executed and a fixed price/liability (debt) is generated, no further increase is permitted — the profit margin must be built into the original sale price before the debt is fixed, not added afterward. This timing distinction (profit margin embedded in the original price vs addition after a debt/liability is fixed) is presented as the central operative test separating legitimate credit-sale profit from Riba.","examExplanation":"Core rule: a creditor (in both Qard/loan and Dayn/debt) may recover only the Ra'asul-mal (principal) — any addition is Riba. The operative test for distinguishing legitimate profit from Riba is timing: a profit margin embedded in the original sale price (before the debt/liability is fixed) is valid; any addition made after the price/debt is fixed (e.g. for late payment) is Riba. Three transaction types with different ownership/risk implications: Bai' (sale) transfers ownership permanently at execution (cash or credit); Qard (loan) transfers only temporary ownership, must be repaid identically, free of any charge; Ijarah (lease) never transfers ownership, only usufruct against rent — and only applies to assets whose corpus is not consumed by use (hence money cannot be leased/rented for interest).","keyPoints":["A Qard (loan) is money/goods taken with an obligation to return the same or similar amount on demand; it must be entirely free of charge in Islamic finance.","A Dayn (debt) is a liability arising from a credit transaction (e.g. credit sale, accrued Ijarah rent); payable at the agreed time, and the creditor cannot demand earlier payment.","In both loans and debts, the creditor's right is limited to the Ra'asul-mal (principal) — any addition, however small, is Riba (Qur'an 2:278-279).","The key timing test: a profit margin may be legitimately embedded in the original sale price (reflecting a deferred payment term) before a debt is fixed; no addition is permitted once the price/liability is fixed (this is what distinguishes valid credit-sale pricing from Riba).","Bai' (sale) transfers ownership permanently and definitively at execution, whether payment is spot or deferred.","Ijarah (lease) never transfers ownership — only the usufruct, against rent — and applies only to assets whose corpus is not consumed/altered by use; this is why money (consumed on use) cannot be leased for 'rent' (interest), while a house or vehicle can be leased for rent."],"definitions":[{"term":"Qard","definition":"A loan: any commodity or amount of money taken from another person with liability to return the same or similar commodity/amount when demanded by the creditor; always free of charge in Islamic finance."},{"term":"Dayn","definition":"A debt: a liability to pay resulting from a credit transaction, such as a sale/purchase on credit or due rentals in Ijarah; payable at a stipulated time, with no right for the creditor to demand earlier payment."},{"term":"Bai'","definition":"Sale — a contract in which ownership of a commodity is transferred to the buyer at the time of execution, permanently and definitively, whether payment is on the spot or deferred."}],"conditions":["A loan (Qard) must be entirely free of charge — the debtor returns exactly the same or similar amount, no more.","A profit margin on a credit sale must be built into the original agreed price before the debt/liability is fixed; no addition is permissible after that point.","Only assets whose corpus/original form is not consumed or altered by use may be leased (Ijarah); items consumed or transformed by use (money, edibles, fuel, yarn) cannot be leased."],"principles":["The creditor's right in both loans and debts is limited to the principal (Ra'asul-mal); anything over and above it is Riba, regardless of amount."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The 'trade is like Riba' objection and its refutation","kind":"textbook","text":"Usurers argued (Qur'an 2:275) that raising a price for a deferred-payment sale is no different from adding a charge to an overdue debt. The Qur'an's answer: 'Allah has permitted trading and prohibited Riba' — once a sale is executed and a fixed price/debt generated, no further increase is allowed, whereas the original sale price itself may legitimately reflect a deferred-payment premium."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think any 'markup' embedded in a credit sale price is itself Riba; the chapter clarifies this is valid (part of the original bargained price), whereas an addition made after the debt is fixed (e.g. for late payment) is Riba — timing, not the mere existence of a markup, is the operative distinction.","'Renting money' (charging interest) is sometimes conflated with legitimate Ijarah (leasing); the chapter explains money cannot be an Ijarah subject at all because it is consumed/changes form on use, unlike a leasable asset such as a house."],"distinctions":["Bai' (permanent ownership transfer, cash or credit) vs Qard (temporary ownership transfer, free of charge, must be returned identically) vs Ijarah (no ownership transfer, only usufruct against rent, only for non-consumable assets)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba al-Nasiah","Salam","Ijarah","Hibah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[47,48,49]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Credit sale price","b":"Interest on a loan","text":"A higher credit price is agreed before the sale is executed, for a commodity whose ownership passes; interest is an excess over a loaned principal. After a credit sale is executed, the price becomes a debt and cannot increase."},{"a":"Rent on a house","b":"Rent on money","text":"A house retains its corpus in use and can be leased; money is consumed in use and cannot be leased."}],"table":{"caption":"How contracts differ (Section 3.2.1, ‘How to Distinguish’)","head":["Contract","Ownership","Return permitted?"],"rows":[["Bai‘ (sale, cash or credit)","Transfers permanently at execution","Yes — profit margin in price"],["Salam","Obligation to deliver/take at stipulated time","Yes — price agreed upfront"],["Hibah (gift)","Transfers permanently, free","No payment"],["Qard (loan)","Temporary; same/similar to be returned","No — any excess is Riba"],["Ijarah (lease)","Remains with lessor; usufruct transferred","Yes — rent; lessor bears ownership risk"]]}},{"id":"t3.2.1.c","section":"3.2.1","title":"Riba: Misconceptions and the Author’s Responses","pages":[49,51],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba"],"intuition":"People offer many arguments that modern interest is not the prohibited Riba. How does the author answer each one?","simpleExplanation":"The author examines arguments that only pre-Islamic Riba, only high rates, only consumption loans, or interest justified by necessity, inflation or rich borrowers is permitted — and rejects each: any addition over principal is Riba, whatever the rate, purpose or borrower.","academicExplanation":["Islam accepts no distinction, for prohibition, between “reasonable” and “exorbitant” rates, or between loans for consumption and production. The author sets out the arguments and his responses; the Shariat Appellate Bench (SAB) of Pakistan’s Supreme Court is cited extensively."],"examExplanation":"Rejected arguments: only Riba al-jahiliyyah prohibited; only excessive rates (3:130); Umar’s alleged doubt; no commercial loans in Arabia; necessity; inflation; rich debtors. Principle: any addition over principal is Riba irrespective of rate, purpose or debtor.","keyPoints":["SAB: permissibility of interest cannot depend on the debtor’s financial position or the purpose of the loan.","Modern interest is worse than Riba al-jahiliyyah: it is charged at inception and on overdue amounts."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"“Only for production loans?”","kind":"textbook","text":"The argument that interest is harmless on business loans is rejected: the Shariat Appellate Bench held that permissibility cannot depend on the debtor’s financial position or the purpose of the loan, and modern interest is charged both at inception and on overdue amounts (pp. 49–51)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islam prohibits usury but allows moderate interest.","right":"The author rejects the usury/interest distinction; any addition over the principal is Riba."}],"relatedTopics":["t17.3","t3.2.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How does the author answer the argument that indexation protects lenders against inflation?","options":["He accepts it","Indexation of financial obligations also leads to Riba","Only for rich lenders","Only for gold loans"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"A loan seeking benefit involves Riba; indexation therefore leads to Riba (p. 51)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"Some Misconceptions About Riba","title":"Common Misconceptions About Riba and Their Rebuttal","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author systematically rebuts several arguments used to claim that modern commercial/bank interest is not the Riba prohibited by Islam.","simpleExplanation":"Several arguments try to carve out modern bank interest from the Riba ban: 'only ancient usury-style compounding counts', 'only high/exploitative rates count', 'only consumption loans count, not business loans', 'interest is just fair compensation for inflation', or 'today's borrowers aren't poor so it's not exploitative'. The author goes through each one and shows, using Qur'anic text, Hadith and historical evidence (including a landmark Pakistani court judgment), why none of them hold up — any addition over principal, at any rate, for any purpose, is Riba.","academicExplanation":"The author catalogs and rebuts several misconceptions: (1) That only 'Riba al-jahiliyyah' (the pre-Islamic practice of a creditor offering, at maturity, either repayment or a larger debt with extended time) is prohibited, and that interest charged from the outset is not Riba — rebutted by noting multiple forms of pre-Islamic Riba (including upfront additions) existed and were all prohibited, and that modern compounding is arguably worse since it charges interest both at inception and on overdue amounts, unlike the historical practice which applied only upon default. (2) That only high/'usurious' rates are prohibited, with 'normal' rates permissible (citing a narrow reading of 3:130's 'doubled and redoubled') — rebutted because the Qur'an (2:279) limits creditors to the principal in absolute terms ('if you repent, you have your principal only'), 'rate' is inherently relative and any rate compounds over time, and the Encyclopaedia of Islam analogy is invoked: just as 5:44 prohibiting selling Allah's Signs 'for a low price' obviously doesn't permit selling them at a high price, 3:130's mention of 'doubled and redoubled' Riba does not imply smaller increases are permitted. (3) That the Prophet died before clarifying disputed cases about Riba's meaning (citing a report about Umar) — rebutted via the Shariat Appellate Bench (SAB) of Pakistan's Supreme Court, which found Umar had no doubt about the prohibition of Riba al-Nasiah as it applies to modern commercial transactions. (4) That commercial/productive loans (as opposed to consumption loans) were not the target of the original prohibition, since interest-bearing commercial lending was allegedly unknown in Arabia at the time — rebutted at length by the SAB, which documented that commercial, industrial and agricultural interest-based loans were prevalent in the neighbouring Byzantine Empire (with Emperor Justinian, 527-565 AD, even legislating permissible interest rates for different borrower types) and that Makkan Arab traders had extensive commercial relations with Byzantine Syria — concluding permissibility of interest cannot depend on the debtor's financial position or the loan's purpose. (5) The 'Principle of Necessity' argument for an interest-based system — rebutted by citing interest's documented adverse macroeconomic impact, especially on developing countries. (6) That interest is justified as compensation for inflation/loss of purchasing power — rebutted on the logic that currency depreciation affects money in the lender's pocket exactly as it would money already lent; charging extra specifically to offset one's own expected loss from holding money still constitutes seeking a benefit from lending (any loan that seeks benefit involves Riba), so indexation of debts to inflation, GNP growth, gold prices etc. is itself ruled Riba (a position the OIC Fiqh Academy is said to have categorically confirmed, while permitting lending in a currency/gold considered less inflation-vulnerable, without allowing the debt liability itself to increase). (7) That today's bank borrowers are not poor, so charging them interest isn't exploitative — rebutted as actually strengthening the case against interest: relatively richer borrowers obtain cheap funds relative to their profits, pass on the interest cost (as an expense) to consumers, and if a loss occurs they must still repay in full, sometimes driving them to unethical practices; the exploitative effect operates on society (savers, depositors, the public) regardless of the individual borrower's wealth.","examExplanation":"Seven rebutted misconceptions about Riba: (1) 'only pre-Islamic roll-over-style Riba counts' — false, multiple forms were prohibited, and modern compounding is arguably worse. (2) 'only high/usurious rates are Riba' — false, per Qur'an 2:279 (principal only) and the 'low price'/'high price' analogy from 5:44. (3) 'the Prophet left the definition unsettled' — refuted by Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench citing Umar's clarity on Riba al-Nasiah. (4) 'only consumption loans were targeted, not commercial loans' — refuted by SAB's historical evidence of Byzantine commercial interest lending known to Makkan traders. (5) 'Principle of Necessity' justifies interest — rejected given interest's documented harm, especially to developing economies. (6) 'interest merely compensates for inflation' — rejected; indexation of debt to inflation/prices is itself ruled a form of Riba by the OIC Fiqh Academy. (7) 'today's borrowers aren't poor, so it's not exploitative' — rejected; this actually strengthens the anti-interest case, since rich borrowers pass interest costs to consumers while depositors/savers still bear the systemic exploitation.","keyPoints":["Riba al-jahiliyyah (roll-over-at-maturity Riba) was not the only pre-Islamic form prohibited; modern compounding (interest charged both at inception and on overdue amounts) is arguably a more severe form.","The Qur'an (2:279) limits creditors to the principal absolutely; 'rate' is relative and any positive rate compounds over time, so the 'only high rates are Riba' argument fails — illustrated by the analogy that prohibiting selling Allah's Signs 'for a low price' (5:44) obviously doesn't permit selling them at a high price.","Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench found extensive historical evidence that commercial/productive interest-based loans (e.g. in the Byzantine Empire, known to Makkan traders) were prevalent when Riba was prohibited, refuting the claim the prohibition targeted only consumption loans.","Indexing debt obligations to inflation, GNP growth, gold prices or similar benchmarks is itself ruled a form of Riba by the OIC Fiqh Academy, since it seeks a benefit for the lender from the loan; lending in a less inflation-vulnerable currency/gold is permitted, but the nominal debt liability itself cannot be increased.","The argument that modern borrowers 'aren't poor' actually strengthens the case against interest: richer borrowers pass interest costs on to consumers as an expense, while the interest-based system's exploitative effect operates systemically on savers/depositors regardless of any individual borrower's wealth."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba al-jahiliyyah","definition":"The specific pre-Islamic practice where, at a loan's maturity, the creditor offered the debtor either immediate repayment or an increased debt amount with extended time; some scholars mistakenly argue only this form (not upfront interest) is prohibited."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Any loan that seeks a benefit for the lender (whether framed as interest, a fee, or inflation-indexation) involves Riba — the form of the benefit does not change its substance."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Byzantine commercial interest lending known to Makkan traders","kind":"textbook","text":"Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench documented that commercial, industrial and agricultural interest-based loans were prevalent in the Byzantine Empire ruling Syria — so much so that Emperor Justinian (527-565 AD) legislated permissible interest rates for different borrower types — and that Makkan Arab traders had extensive commercial relations with Byzantine Syria, refuting the claim that interest-bearing commercial lending was unknown to the Prophet's contemporaries."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume 'usury' (high/exploitative rates) and 'interest' (normal/moderate rates) are Shari'ah-meaningful categories; the chapter explicitly rejects this distinction as having no basis in the Qur'anic text, calling it a device used 'to deceive mankind and to allow the same old robbery in a more presentable form.'"],"distinctions":["Usury vs interest (a distinction the chapter says has no Shari'ah basis, though it exists in conventional/legal terminology since England's 1545 usury laws) — both fall under Riba regardless of rate."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba al-Nasiah","Shariat Appellate Bench","OIC Fiqh Academy"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,50,51]}}],"debate":[{"issue":"Only Riba al-jahiliyyah is prohibited","criticism":"Only the pre-Islamic practice of increasing a debt at maturity in exchange for more time is prohibited; a charge added at the start of a loan is not Riba.","response":"Many forms of Riba existed before Islam, including additions over loans and debts, and all were prohibited. Even on that narrow view, time-based compounding falls within it; modern interest is worse since it is charged both at inception and on overdue amounts.","takeaway":"The prohibition covers any addition over the principal, not one historical form."},{"issue":"Only high (usurious) rates are prohibited","criticism":"Verse 3:130 mentions “doubled and redoubled”, so only excessive, exploitative rates are Riba; modern bank rates are reasonable.","response":"The Qur’an says “you have your principal only”; any addition is Riba. “Rate” is relative — any rate doubles the principal over time. Tabari: creditors get only the original amount. The Urdu Encyclopaedia of Islam’s analogy: “sell not the signs of Allah for a low price” does not permit selling them for a high price.","takeaway":"Prohibition is irrespective of the rate."},{"issue":"Umar did not know the meaning of Riba","criticism":"Umar said the Prophet (pbuh) passed away before clarifying Riba.","response":"The SAB concluded that Umar had not the slightest doubt about the prohibition of Riba Al-Nasiah, which is involved in modern commercial interest.","takeaway":"Any uncertainty concerned details, not loan interest."},{"issue":"No commercial interest existed in Arabia","criticism":"Only consumption loans were known; the prohibition does not cover productive loans.","response":"The SAB found commercial and productive loans were known to Arabs; the Byzantine Empire (with which Makkan caravans traded) had commercial interest regulated by Justinian. Permissibility cannot depend on the debtor’s position or loan purpose.","takeaway":"No consumption vs production distinction."},{"issue":"Necessity","criticism":"Interest-based systems are needed under the principle of necessity.","response":"The adverse impact of interest makes it the biggest threat to developing economies, a view held by many economists.","takeaway":"Necessity does not justify a system-wide interest regime."},{"issue":"Inflation","criticism":"Interest compensates for loss of purchasing power.","response":"Depreciation affects money equally in the lender’s pocket and with the borrower; a loan seeking benefit involves Riba, so indexation of financial obligations also leads to Riba.","takeaway":"Indexation is not a permissible substitute."},{"issue":"Debtors today are rich","criticism":"Charging rich borrowers interest is not unjust.","response":"This strengthens the case against interest: the rich obtain cheap funds, pass interest to consumers as an expense, and the rich get richer; interest exploits whichever party loses.","takeaway":"Interest is prohibited regardless of who is exploited."}]},{"id":"t3.2.1.d","section":"3.2.1","title":"Riba in Sale/Exchange Transactions (Riba Al-Fadl and Bai‘ al Sarf)","pages":[51,52],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba-fadl","bai-sarf","illah"],"intuition":"Can Riba occur without any loan? Yes — when like is exchanged for like unequally or with delay.","simpleExplanation":"The Hadith of six commodities says gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, dates for dates, salt for salt must be equal and hand-to-hand. If the kinds differ, you may sell as you wish, but still hand-to-hand. Today the rule applies to anything used as money — including currencies.","academicExplanation":["Riba in exchange contracts is Riba Al-Fadl, based on the six-commodities Hadith and the dates traditions (sell the lower-quality dates for money, then buy the better ones). Exchange rules differ by contract and asset type: goods are traded at market prices; gold, silver and monetary units (Athman) are governed by Bai‘ al Sarf; usufructs and services by Ijarah/Ujrah; loans and debts by rules of repayment and assignment. Jurists extended the rules by Qiyas using the ‘Illah (effective cause): unit of value (Thamaniyyah) and edibility.","Imam Nawavi’s summary: (1) when the ‘Illah of the two goods differs, excess and delay are both permissible (gold for wheat; dollars for a car); (2) when the commodities are the same, excess and delay are both prohibited (gold for gold, dollars for dollars); (3) when commodities differ but the ‘Illah is the same (gold for silver; US Dollars for Yen; wheat for rice), excess is allowed but delay is not. In the present scenario the major ‘Illah is use in lieu of money: there is consensus that Riba rules apply to anything serving as money — gold, silver, paper currency or IOUs. (This prohibition concerns sale transactions; Qard and Dayn are exempt from this rule.)"],"examExplanation":"Riba Al-Fadl: excess in exchange of same genus (six commodities). Rules (Nawavi): different ‘Illah → excess and delay allowed; same commodity → both prohibited; different commodity, same ‘Illah → excess allowed, delay not. ‘Illah today: use as money — applies to all currencies.","keyPoints":["Six commodities: gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, salt.","Like for like, equal for equal, hand to hand.","‘Illah: Thamaniyyah (unit of value) and edibility.","Rules apply to anything functioning as money, including currencies — important for foreign exchange."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba Al-Fadl","definition":"Quality premium/excess in the exchange of low-quality for better-quality goods of the same genus (e.g. dates for dates)."},{"term":"Bai‘ al Sarf","definition":"Sale of monetary units (Athman) — gold, silver, currencies — governed by specific exchange rules."},{"term":"‘Illah","definition":"Effective cause of a ruling, used to extend it by analogy (e.g. Thamaniyyah, edibility)."},{"term":"Athman","definition":"Monetary units / media of exchange such as gold, silver or currencies."}],"examples":[{"title":"Bilal’s dates","kind":"textbook","text":"Bilal exchanged two measures of lower-quality dates for one of high quality. The Prophet (pbuh) called it the forbidden Riba and advised selling the first dates for money and using the proceeds to buy the better ones."},{"title":"Money as the modern ‘Illah","kind":"textbook","text":"There is consensus that Riba rules apply to anything serving the function of money — gold, silver, paper currency or IOUs (p. 52)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Riba applies only to loans.","right":"Riba Al-Fadl arises in exchanges of the same genus, and delay in exchanging currencies involves Riba too."},{"wrong":"Two different currencies can be exchanged with delayed settlement.","right":"Currencies share the ‘Illah of money: excess (any rate) is allowed but delay is not."},{"wrong":"The equal-and-spot rule for like goods also applies to loans (Qard).","right":"The rule is for sale/exchange contracts; non-remunerative contracts like Qard and Dayn are exempt from it (p. 52, n. 21)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.5","t4.7.2","t6.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"US Dollars are exchanged for Japanese Yen. Which rule applies?","options":["Excess and delay both allowed","Excess allowed, delay not allowed","Both prohibited","Only equal amounts allowed"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Different commodities with the same ‘Illah (medium of exchange): excess allowed but delay prohibited (p. 52)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"Riba in Sale/Exchange Transactions and Connotation of the Term Riba","title":"Riba al-Fadl: Riba in Sale/Exchange Transactions, and the Full Connotation of Riba","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author explains Riba al-Fadl (Riba in exchange of like commodities), the classical 'six commodities' Hadith and its exchange rules, and synthesizes a comprehensive definition covering both loan-based and exchange-based Riba.","simpleExplanation":"Beyond interest on loans, Islam also prohibits unequal exchanges of the same type of commodity — you can't trade a small amount of high-quality dates for a larger amount of low-quality dates, because that hidden 'quality premium' is also a form of Riba. The rule (from a famous Hadith listing gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates and salt): if you're exchanging the same commodity type, the amounts must be exactly equal and the exchange must happen on the spot — no delay and no excess allowed. If the two things being exchanged are different (like gold for wheat), these restrictions don't apply — normal market bargaining is fine.","academicExplanation":"The author explains that the last several Hadith cited (the 'six commodities' Hadith and the two dates-exchange incidents involving Bilal and the Khyber Zakat collector) establish Riba al-Fadl — Riba in sale/exchange contracts, distinct from Riba al-Nasiah in loans. The six-commodities Hadith ('Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, dates for dates and salt for salt — like for like, equal for equal, and hand to hand; if the commodities differ, then you may sell as you wish, provided the exchange is hand to hand') is the foundational text; jurists extended its scope to other commodities by Qiyas (analogy) based on the 'Illah (effective legal cause) of the ruling. Two 'Illah are identified for these six commodities: being a unit of value (Thamaniyyah — gold, silver) and edibility (the four foodstuffs). Imam Nawavi's summarized rules (Bai' al Sarf — exchange of monetary units): (a) when the underlying 'Illah of the two exchanged goods differs (e.g. gold for wheat, dollars for a car), both excess/shortfall and delay are permitted; (b) when the commodities are of the same genus (gold for gold, wheat for wheat, dollars for dollars), both excess and delay are prohibited — exchange must be equal and immediate; (c) when the commodities are heterogeneous but share the same 'Illah (e.g. gold for silver, US Dollars for Japanese Yen, wheat for rice — sharing edibility), excess/deficiency is allowed but delay is not. The modern extension: scholarly consensus holds Riba rules apply to anything functioning as money (gold, silver, paper currency, IOUs) — directly relevant to foreign exchange transactions today. The author then synthesizes a comprehensive, functional definition of Riba spanning both loan- and exchange-based forms: 'any increase over and above the principal amount payable in a contract obligation, not covered by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise' — a definition that requires all accruals to correspond to genuine liability/risk, thereby excluding legitimate trading/Shirkah profit, Ujrah/Wakalah service fees, and Ijarah rentals from the Riba prohibition. Justice Wajihuddin Ahmad's (Shariat Appellate Bench) alternative formal definition is also given, emphasizing the three exchange conditions: Saw'am-bi-sawaa' (equal for equal), Mithlam-bi-mithlin (like for like), and Yadam-bi-yadin (hand to hand/on the spot).","examExplanation":"Riba al-Fadl = Riba in sale/exchange transactions (distinct from Riba al-Nasiah in loans/debts), grounded in the 'six commodities' Hadith (gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, salt). Imam Nawavi's rules: same-genus exchange (e.g. gold-for-gold) requires equal quantities and immediate (hand-to-hand) exchange, no excess or delay permitted; different-'Illah exchange (e.g. gold for wheat) has no such restriction; same-'Illah but different-genus exchange (e.g. gold for silver, or US Dollars for Yen) permits excess/deficiency but not delay. Modern application: these rules extend by Qiyas to anything functioning as money (currencies, IOUs) — directly relevant to FX transactions. Comprehensive Riba definition: any increase over principal not matched by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise — this is why trading profit, service fees (Ujrah), and Ijarah rentals are excluded from Riba.","keyPoints":["Riba al-Fadl (Riba in sale/exchange) is grounded in the 'six commodities' Hadith: gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, salt — 'like for like, equal for equal, hand to hand'; differing commodities may be exchanged freely but still hand to hand.","Two 'Illah (effective causes) underlie the six-commodities rule: Thamaniyyah (being a unit of value — gold, silver) and edibility (the four foodstuffs); jurists extended the rule to other items sharing these causes via Qiyas.","Imam Nawavi's three-tier rule: (1) different 'Illah — excess and delay both permitted; (2) same genus (same 'Illah and same commodity) — excess and delay both prohibited (must be equal and immediate); (3) same 'Illah, different genus — excess/deficiency permitted, but delay prohibited.","Scholarly consensus extends Riba rules to anything functioning as money — gold, silver, paper currency, IOUs — which is directly relevant to modern foreign exchange transactions.","Comprehensive definition: Riba is any increase over the principal not matched by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise — this definitional test is what excludes legitimate trading profit, Ujrah/Wakalah fees and Ijarah rentals from being classified as Riba."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba al-Fadl","definition":"The quality premium in exchange of low-quality for better-quality goods of the same genus (e.g. dates for dates, wheat for wheat) — an excess in the exchange of Ribawi goods of a single genus on both sides."},{"term":"Riba al-Nasiah","definition":"Riba of delay — an increment on the principal of a loan or debt payable, arising because the exchange of counter-values is not immediate; this is the form present in all modern interest-based banking transactions (also termed Riba al-Qur'an)."},{"term":"Bai' al Sarf","definition":"The sale/exchange of gold, silver or other monetary units (Athman) on both sides, governed by specific rules requiring equality and immediacy when the exchanged units are of the same genus."},{"term":"Thamaniyyah","definition":"The quality of serving as a unit/medium of value (as gold and silver do), one of the two 'Illah (effective causes) underlying the Riba al-Fadl rules for the six named commodities."}],"conditions":["Exchange of the same genus of Ribawi commodity (e.g. gold for gold, wheat for wheat) must be equal in quantity and hand-to-hand (immediate) — no excess and no delay permitted.","Exchange of different genus but same 'Illah commodities (e.g. gold for silver, one currency for another) permits excess/deficiency but not delay.","Exchange of commodities with entirely different 'Illah (e.g. gold for wheat) permits both excess/deficiency and delay."],"principles":["Comprehensive Riba definition: any increase over and above the principal amount payable in a contract obligation, not matched by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bilal's dates exchange","kind":"textbook","text":"Bilal traded two volumes of lower-quality dates for one volume of higher-quality dates. The Prophet told him: 'This is precisely the forbidden Riba! ... Sell the first type of dates, and use the proceeds to buy the others' — illustrating that even a seemingly fair barter of unequal quality/quantity within the same commodity is Riba al-Fadl, and the correct approach is to sell for cash first, then buy the desired item separately."}],"commonConfusions":["Riba al-Fadl is sometimes dismissed as an archaic rule with no modern relevance; the chapter notes its extension (via Qiyas, to anything functioning as money) remains directly relevant to modern foreign exchange and currency transactions."],"distinctions":["Riba al-Nasiah (delay-based, loans/debts) vs Riba al-Fadl (quantity/quality-based, same-genus exchange transactions) — both are forms of Riba but arise from different transaction types."],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al Sarf","Qiyas","Ribawi goods"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52,53]}}],"table":{"caption":"Exchange rules (Imam Nawavi’s summary, p. 52)","head":["Case","Example","Excess","Delay"],"rows":[["Different ‘Illah","Gold for wheat; dollars for a car","Allowed","Allowed"],["Same commodity","Gold for gold; wheat for wheat; dollars for dollars","Prohibited","Prohibited"],["Different commodity, same ‘Illah","Gold for silver; USD for JPY; wheat for rice","Allowed","Prohibited"]]},"calc":{"type":"exchange-rule"}},{"id":"t3.2.1.e","section":"3.2.1","title":"Connotation of the Term Riba","pages":[52,53],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","riba-nasiah","riba-fadl"],"intuition":"How should you define Riba precisely in an exam — covering loans, sales and all modern transactions?","simpleExplanation":"Riba Al-Nasiah is excess from delay (loans — also called Riba Al-Qur’an); Riba Al-Fadl is excess in exchanging like goods. A broad definition: any increase over the principal in a contract obligation not matched by an increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise.","academicExplanation":["Literally Riba means excess; in Shari’ah it is an addition, however slight, over the principal of a loan or debt. Nasiah means delay; Riba Al-Nasiah is the benefit or excess from delay of a counter value in an exchange based on loans or sales — the potential benefit over the stipulated delay period. It relates to loans and is also termed Riba Al-Qur’an. Riba Al-Fadl is the quality premium in exchanging goods of the same genus. Riba therefore includes both usury and interest; conventional interest-bearing loans involve both Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl.","Broader definition: “Riba means and includes any increase over and above the principal amount payable in a contract obligation, not covered by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise.” This requires accruals to correspond to liability and risk and excludes profit in trading and Shirkah, commission/service charges in Ujrah and Wakalah, and rentals in Ijarah. Justice Wajihuddin Ahmad (SAB) defined Riba as every return arising purely in consideration of time for the use of money or other things of value lent, and every increase in exchange of goods violating “equal for equal”, “like for like” and “hand to hand”."],"examExplanation":"Riba Al-Nasiah (Riba Al-Qur’an): excess from delay — loans/debts. Riba Al-Fadl: excess in same-genus exchange. Broad definition: any increase over principal in a contract obligation not covered by corresponding labour, commodity, risk or expertise.","keyPoints":["Riba Al-Nasiah = Riba Al-Qur’an = loan Riba.","Conventional interest-bearing loans involve both types.","Excluded from Riba: trading/Shirkah profit, Ujrah/Wakalah fees, Ijarah rentals.","Justice Wajihuddin Ahmad’s definition covers returns purely for time on money lent and increases violating “equal for equal, like for like, hand to hand”."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba Al-Nasiah","definition":"Benefit or excess arising from delay of a counter value in an exchange based on loans or sales; also called Riba Al-Qur’an."},{"term":"Riba (broad definition)","definition":"“Any increase over and above the principal amount payable in a contract obligation, not covered by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise” (definition quoted in the textbook)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Nasiah and Fadl in one loan","kind":"practice","text":"Lending 1,000 dollars today for 1,050 dollars in a year involves Riba al-Nasiah (the delay premium) and Riba al-Fadl (unequal exchange of like money), which is why the book says interest-bearing loans involve both (pp. 52–53)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Conventional loan interest involves only Riba Al-Nasiah.","right":"The author says loan interest involves both Riba Al-Nasiah (benefit from delay) and Riba Al-Fadl (extra money paid when due) (p. 53)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.d","t17.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is NOT Riba under the broad definition?","options":["An addition to a loan for extra time","Rent charged in Ijarah","An excess when exchanging gold for gold","Indexing a loan to inflation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Rentals in Ijarah correspond to liability and risk of ownership and are excluded (p. 53)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t6","ch3-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"The Rationale for the Prohibition of Interest","title":"The Rationale for Prohibiting Interest: Distributive Justice and Economic Evidence","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author identifies distributive justice as the most convincing rationale for the prohibition of interest, explains why interest on productive (not just consumption) loans is also prohibited, and marshals extensive empirical/economic evidence of interest's harmful effects.","simpleExplanation":"Different thinkers give different reasons for banning interest (fairness, preventing luxury, avoiding injustice), but the author says the strongest reason is distributive justice: interest lets wealth concentrate among the already-wealthy instead of 'circulating' broadly, which the Qur'an explicitly warns against. Even interest on business loans (not just personal/consumption loans) is unfair, because the lender gets a guaranteed return while the entrepreneur bears all the uncertainty — real fairness requires the capital provider to share in the risk, not just collect a fixed fee regardless of outcome. The author also cites extensive real-world evidence (savings behavior, debt-to-equity ratios, banking crises, Third World debt) showing interest doesn't even deliver the economic benefits (more savings, more productive investment) that conventional economists claim for it.","academicExplanation":"The author surveys candidate rationales for prohibiting interest — socio-economic/distributive justice, intergenerational equity, economic instability, ecological destruction — and settles on distributive justice as the most convincing, since Riba's prohibition is intended to prevent wealth accumulating in a few hands, i.e. to stop wealth from 'circulating among the rich' (Qur'an 59:7). He rejects three narrower or purely-ritual rationales: (a) that prohibition is simply a matter of ritual obedience requiring no rationale; (b) that it prevents only luxury/hoarding; (c) that Zulm (injustice/exploitation) alone is the cause, which some use to argue modern 'non-exploitative' commercial interest should be exempt. He argues interest on productive loans is prohibited too (not just consumption loans), because a productive investment's profit is inherently uncertain (with possibility of loss), so a fixed, predetermined interest rate cannot be morally justified — justice requires the capital provider to share risk with the entrepreneur to earn any return. This yields a foundational difference from capitalism: capitalism treats money capital like labour and land (each entitled to a return regardless of profit/loss); Islam instead treats money capital on a par with enterprise (entitled only to risk-contingent profit). He repeats Yusuf Ali's translation/commentary on Qur'an verse 2:275, framing legitimate trade as building prosperity while usury breeds 'idlers, cruel bloodsuckers' — and argues the 'usury vs interest' rate-based distinction is meaningless, since any positive rate compounds over time and eventually produces exploitation (evidenced, he argues, by developing countries' chronic problems: low savings, heavy deficits, inflation-cum-recession, high debt servicing, unemployment). He challenges the conventional claim that interest promotes savings/investment/development, arguing savings levels depend far more on income levels, income distribution, inflation and fiscal policy than on the interest rate; he also disputes that borrowing enhances productivity/repayment capacity. Empirical evidence cited: Kester's (1986) finding that debt-to-equity ratios in major US/Japanese business categories are substantially below unity (i.e. equity financing is more prevalent than debt financing, even more so without debt's artificial tax advantage); Mohsin S. Khan's (1986) finding that interest-based credit increases banking-crisis risk, since sufficiently collateralized banks will finance even poor-feasibility projects, leading to business failures. He cites the historical use of debt as a geopolitical control tool (the Ottoman Empire's subjugation via interest-based debt; the IMF/World Bank allegedly used post-9/11 as tools of US foreign policy, per an Economist headline 'Bribing Allies'), and quotes James Robertson describing how the interest system systematically transfers money from those who have less to those who have more (because larger lenders earn more interest, smaller/poorer parties borrow more, and interest costs are embedded in the price of all goods/services) — concluding the case for an interest-free, inflation-free 21st-century money system is strong. He notes interest generates unemployment by diverting capital toward risk-free government paper rather than productive investment requiring labour/risk (e.g. building a factory), producing recession, bankruptcies and stagflation, and cites Visser and McIntosh's conclusion that the modern global economy is more usury-based than ever, suggesting Western 'ethical' investors have more to learn from Islamic finance than generally acknowledged.","examExplanation":"The author's chosen rationale for prohibiting interest: distributive justice (preventing wealth from 'circulating only among the rich' — Qur'an 59:7), not merely ritual obedience, luxury-prevention, or injustice-avoidance alone. Interest on productive/commercial loans is prohibited (not just consumption loans) because a productive venture's outcome is inherently uncertain, so a fixed guaranteed return cannot be morally justified — capital must share risk with the entrepreneur to earn any return. This creates the foundational Islam-vs-capitalism divide: capitalism entitles money capital to a return like labour/land (regardless of outcome); Islam treats money capital like enterprise (risk-contingent profit only). Empirical evidence cited against interest's supposed benefits: Kester (1986) — debt-to-equity ratios below unity in major US/Japan business categories (equity financing dominates); Mohsin S. Khan (1986) — interest-based credit increases banking-crisis risk via over-collateralized lending to poor-feasibility projects.","keyPoints":["The author identifies distributive justice as the most convincing rationale for prohibiting interest: it prevents wealth from being confined to circulation only 'among the rich' (Qur'an 59:7).","Interest on productive/commercial loans (not just consumption loans) is also prohibited, since a business's profit outcome is inherently uncertain — a fixed, predetermined interest rate on such a loan cannot be morally justified; the capital provider must share risk to earn any return.","Foundational Islam-vs-capitalism distinction: capitalism entitles money capital to a return like labour and land, regardless of profit or loss; Islam treats money capital on a par with enterprise — entitled only to risk-contingent profit.","The 'usury vs interest' rate-based distinction is dismissed as meaningless, since any positive interest rate compounds over time into exploitation, and 'reasonable' vs 'usurious' shifts with inflation.","The conventional claim that interest drives savings/investment/development is disputed: savings depend more on income levels, distribution, inflation and fiscal policy than on interest rates; Kester (1986) shows equity financing (not debt) dominates major US/Japanese business categories; Mohsin S. Khan (1986) shows interest-based credit raises banking-crisis risk.","James Robertson's analysis is cited to argue the interest system systematically transfers wealth from those who have less to those who have more, since larger lenders earn more interest, smaller/poorer parties borrow more, and interest costs are embedded in the price of all goods and services."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Justice demands that a provider of money capital share risk with the entrepreneur if he wishes to earn a profit — a fixed, predetermined interest rate on productive-purpose loans cannot be morally justified given the venture's inherently uncertain outcome."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Kester (1986) debt-to-equity evidence","kind":"textbook","text":"Kester's study of debt-to-equity ratios for major business categories in the US and Japan found most ratios substantially below unity (i.e. equity financing more prevalent than debt), a gap that would be even larger without debt-financing's artificial tax advantage (interest being tax-deductible) — used by the author to rebut the claim that credit/debt plays a critical role in modern investment financing."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islam only prohibits interest on personal/consumption loans and permits it for business/commercial financing; the chapter explicitly rejects this, since the risk-uncertainty rationale applies equally (if not more) to productive loans."],"distinctions":["Capitalism (money capital entitled to a fixed return like labour/land, regardless of business outcome) vs Islamic economics (money capital treated like enterprise — entitled only to a risk-contingent, uncertain profit)."],"relatedConcepts":["Distributive justice","Risk-sharing","Riba al-Nasiah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[54,55,56,57]}},{"id":"ch3-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"Riba and Other Factor Payments","title":"Riba and Factor Payments: Why Money-Capital Cannot Earn a Guaranteed Return","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author closes the Riba discussion by explaining, in factor-of-production terms, why money capital specifically (unlike land or labour) cannot earn a guaranteed return in Islamic finance, and must instead share the risk of loss to be entitled to any profit.","simpleExplanation":"The guaranteed return that interest gives to capital is the key difference between Islamic and conventional finance's treatment of money. In Islamic economics, money capital can only be used productively by being spent/consumed in a real transaction (via a valid sale or lease structure) — you can't just 'rent it out' the way you rent a building. So if someone wants a return on money they've invested, they must bear the risk of loss, exactly like an entrepreneur — otherwise their money simply doesn't earn anything.","academicExplanation":"The author frames the guaranteed reward on capital (interest) as the fundamental point of divergence between Islamic and conventional views of money. In Islamic finance, 'capital' as a factor of production consists of things usable in production only if wholly consumed (historically gold/silver, now money) — such items cannot be lent or leased; the only way to derive benefit from money is to exchange it for goods/services through a valid sale or lease contract structure. Consequently, capital is entitled to profit only if it also bears the liability of loss risk. This is contrasted with the other factor payments: fixed assets (buildings, machinery) claim rent; labour (all physical/mental human effort) is entitled to wages/salaries; the entrepreneur is rewarded for physical/mental exertion, not for bearing capital-loss risk as such — Islam attributes the risk of loss specifically to capital (money) itself. The practical upshot: a person who wants to invest money in a business must accept the risk of loss to be entitled to profit; the land provider gets rent, the labour provider gets wages — and if a joint venture fails, the capital provider loses money, the land provider loses rent, and the labour provider loses wages (i.e. each factor's compensation is contingent on that factor's specific role, and only capital's compensation is contingent on the venture's overall success/failure). If one person owns/runs the entire business alone, they receive or lose all three rewards themselves. The author cross-references Chapter 2 for further detail on these factor-of-production distinctions.","examExplanation":"The fundamental Islam-vs-conventional finance divergence: conventional finance gives capital a guaranteed reward (interest) like labour/land; Islamic finance ties capital's reward (profit) to bearing the risk of loss — money itself cannot be lent/leased for a fee, only exchanged via valid sale/lease structures. If a joint venture fails: the capital provider loses their money, the land provider loses rent, the labour provider loses wages — each factor's loss is specific to its own contribution, but only capital's return is contingent on overall business success (this is why 'no risk, no gain' applies specifically and distinctively to capital).","keyPoints":["The guaranteed reward on capital (interest) marks the fundamental difference between how Islamic and conventional systems treat money as a factor of production.","Money-capital in Islamic finance can be used productively only if wholly consumed/exchanged via a valid sale or lease contract structure — it cannot itself be lent or leased for a fee.","Capital is entitled to profit only if it also bears liability for the risk of loss — this is the specific 'no risk, no gain' rule attributed to capital (money) itself, distinct from land (entitled to rent) and labour (entitled to wages).","If a joint business fails: the capital provider loses their money, the land provider loses rent, and the labour provider loses wages — each factor bears loss specific to its own role, but only capital's return is genuinely contingent on the business's overall success."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Money capital may be used productively and earn a return only through exchange via a valid sale or lease contract structure — it cannot be lent or leased directly for a fee."],"principles":["A person who wants to invest money in any business must take the risk of loss; only then is he entitled to profit."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think 'capital' broadly (including physical/fixed assets) cannot earn any fixed payment in Islamic finance; the chapter clarifies fixed assets like buildings/machinery can earn rent (they are leasable, unlike money), while only money-capital's return must be risk-contingent."],"distinctions":["Capital (money — return contingent on risk-bearing, cannot be leased) vs Land/fixed assets (entitled to rent, can be leased) vs Labour (entitled to wages) vs Entrepreneur (rewarded for physical/mental exertion) — four distinct factor-payment logics in Islamic economics."],"relatedConcepts":["Factors of production","Mudarabah","Musharakah","Ijarah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[57]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Riba Al-Nasiah","b":"Riba Al-Fadl","text":"Nasiah arises from delay (loans/debts, and delayed exchange); Fadl is excess in quantity/quality when exchanging the same genus."}]},{"id":"t3.2.1.f","section":"3.2.1","title":"Rationale for Prohibition, Other Religions and Factor Payments","pages":[53,57],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["riba","distributive-justice","risk-sharing"],"intuition":"Why prohibit interest even on productive loans between willing parties? The author’s answer: distributive justice and risk-sharing.","simpleExplanation":"All revealed religions prohibited interest. The author says the most convincing reason is distributive justice — preventing wealth from circulating only among the rich. For productive loans, profit is uncertain, so a fixed return is unjust; the capital provider should share the risk.","academicExplanation":["Interest is repugnant to all revealed religions; ancient philosophers and the Old and New Testaments prohibited it. Riba was erroneously divided into a permitted cost of capital and a prohibited high rate: in England in 1545 a legal maximum was termed interest and anything above it usury; Calvin and Molinaeus launched the theoretical acceptance of interest in the mid-16th century, gaining momentum with the Industrial Revolution.","Rationale: socio-economic and distributive justice, intergenerational equity, stability and ecology are cited; the author finds distributive justice the only convincing reason — preventing wealth circulating among the rich (59:7). Treating the prohibition as ritual obedience only, or injustice (Zulm) only, is insufficient; the latter is used to distinguish interest from usury. Interest on productive loans is inequitable because profit is unknown and loss possible; justice demands the capital provider share risk. Capitalism treats money capital like labour and land (fixed return regardless of outcome); Islam treats money capital on a par with enterprise.","Interest does not play the claimed role in savings (income level, distribution, inflation, stability and fiscal measures matter more); equity is more prevalent than debt in US and Japanese business finance (Kester, 1986); interest-based credit increases the risk of banking crises (Mohsin Khan, 1986) and collateral-based lending finances poor projects. Interest systematically transfers money from poor to rich (James Robertson), generates unemployment by attracting capital to risk-free paper, and has been used as a tool of control.","Factor payments: capital (things wholly consumed in production — money) cannot be lent or leased for a return; it earns profit only with liability for loss. Fixed assets earn rent; labour earns wages. If a joint business fails, the capital provider loses money, the land provider loses rent and labour loses salary."],"examExplanation":"Rationale: distributive justice (59:7) — the author’s preferred reason. Productive loans: profit uncertain → fixed return unjust; money capital treated on a par with enterprise, must share risk. Interest not needed for savings/investment; equity dominates business finance; interest raises crisis risk and transfers wealth to the rich.","keyPoints":["All revealed religions prohibited interest.","1545 England: split into legal “interest” and “usury”.","Distributive justice is the author’s key rationale.","Money capital is on a par with enterprise, not with land/labour.","Any rate above zero leads to exploitation in the long run."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Why invest in a factory?","kind":"textbook","text":"Why would anyone build a factory and deal with labour problems when risk-free government paper pays a high return? Interest pulls capital towards risk-free returns, causing recession, unemployment and stagflation (p. 57)."},{"title":"The 1545 split of usury and interest","kind":"textbook","text":"The King (and Church) of England in 1545 divided Riba into a legal maximum called “interest” and the excess called usury; Calvin and Molinaeus later argued for interest in theory (p. 54)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Interest is essential for savings and investment.","right":"Savings depend more on income level, distribution, inflation, stability and fiscal policy; empirical evidence shows equity, not debt, funds most business in the US and Japan (pp. 55–56)."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.5.3","t1.5","t4.2.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which rationale for the prohibition of Riba does the author find most convincing?","options":["Ritual obedience only","Distributive justice — preventing wealth accumulation in few hands","Preventing luxury","Ecological concerns"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Distributive justice, based on 59:7 (pp. 54–55)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.1","sectionTitle":"Prohibition of Riba in Other Revealed Religions","title":"Riba/Usury Across Revealed Religions and the Historical Usury-versus-Interest Debate","covers":["t3.2.1","t3.2.1.b","t3.2.1.c","t3.2.1.d","t3.2.1.e","t3.2.1.f"],"overview":"The author shows the prohibition of interest is not unique to Islam — it appears in classical philosophy and other revealed religions — and traces how the West's 'usury vs interest' distinction historically emerged to justify what was originally prohibited outright.","simpleExplanation":"Ancient Greek and Roman philosophers, and both the Old and New Testaments, also condemned interest/usury — Islam is not alone in this. Over time in the West, the meaning of 'usury' was narrowed (originally meaning any interest at all, later redefined to mean only 'excessively high' interest), starting with a legal split in 1545 England, and pushed further by Reformation-era theorists and the Industrial Revolution's demand for investment capital — a shift the author sees as historically manufactured to justify what religion originally banned outright, not a principled distinction.","academicExplanation":"The author states Islam is not alone in prohibiting Riba: the institution of interest was rejected by the majority of ancient Greek/Roman philosophers, and both the Old and New Testaments similarly prohibit it — 'from a purely religious point of view there have never been two views about its prohibition,' and no revealed religion accepted 'interest' as a legitimate cost of capital in the conventional economic sense. He gives a common underlying logic across religions: interest reinforces wealth accumulation in fewer hands, increases risk-free relative to risk-related capital, causing business failure, unemployment, and ultimately gross income/wealth inequality leading to social strife and economic chaos. He then traces the historical narrowing of the prohibition in the West: 'usury' (from the Latin for 'use', i.e. price paid for the use of borrowed money) was originally synonymous with any interest; in medieval times 'interest' emerged as a supposedly distinct, lesser concept. The legal split dates to 1545, when the King (and Church) of England divided charges into a 'legal maximum' termed interest and anything above it retained as prohibited usury — a distinction the author frames as originating in specific historical/legal convenience (compounded by the rise of paper/fiduciary money), not Shari'ah-type principle. The theoretical justification movement was launched by Calvin and Molinaeus in the mid-16th century, gained momentum with expanding trade/commerce (justifying interest at least for commercial/productive loans) and was decisively entrenched by the 18th-century Industrial Revolution, ultimately overshadowing religious objections. He quotes the EH.Net Encyclopedia noting usury today (in the West) is legally defined as interest charged without risk to the lender above a legally set rate — with ongoing moral debate since both the Bible and Qur'an can be read as forbidding usury outright — and J.L. Hanson's Dictionary of Commerce and Economics, describing 16th-century English usury laws capping interest at 5% (following the law of Moses and Greek philosophers), later loosened. The chapter's implicit conclusion: the 'usury vs interest' distinction is a historically contingent legal/commercial convenience, not a principled religious or economic distinction — the original, universal religious position condemned any charge for the use of money outright.","examExplanation":"Interest/Riba prohibition is not unique to Islam: rejected by most ancient Greek/Roman philosophers and prohibited in both Old and New Testaments. The West's 'usury vs interest' distinction (usury = excessive/illegal rates; interest = legal/acceptable rates) traces to a specific 1545 English legal division (King and Church setting a 'legal maximum'), theoretically justified from the mid-16th century (Calvin, Molinaeus) and entrenched by the 18th-century Industrial Revolution's demand for investment capital — the author frames this as a historically contingent legal convenience that overshadowed, rather than refuted, the original religious prohibition.","keyPoints":["The prohibition of interest is common to classical Greek/Roman philosophy and to both the Old and New Testaments — Islam is not unique in this position.","The underlying rationale common across religions: interest reinforces wealth concentration, expands risk-free relative to risk-related capital, and drives business failure, unemployment and inequality leading to social/economic instability.","The legal 'usury vs interest' distinction (usury = above a legal maximum, prohibited; interest = below it, permitted) dates specifically to a 1545 English legal division by the King and Church, not to any principled Shari'ah-type distinction.","The theoretical justification for charging interest was launched by Calvin and Molinaeus in the mid-16th century, gaining momentum with expanding trade and becoming entrenched with the 18th-century Industrial Revolution's demand for investment capital.","Both the Bible and the Qur'an can be read as forbidding usury/interest outright — the EH.Net Encyclopedia notes ongoing moral debate in the West about whether any interest-charging is permissible, despite the modern legal narrowing of 'usury' to only excessive rates."],"definitions":[{"term":"Usury (historical/etymological)","definition":"From the Latin for 'use' — originally the price paid for the use of borrowed money (any interest whatsoever); in modern Western legal usage, narrowed to mean only interest charged above a legally set maximum rate."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"1545 English usury law split","kind":"textbook","text":"The King and Church of England divided interest charges into a 'legal maximum' termed interest (permitted) and anything above it retained as prohibited usury — the historical origin of the modern usury/interest distinction, later loosened further; 16th-century English usury laws are also noted to have capped interest at 5%."}],"commonConfusions":["The modern legal distinction between 'usury' (illegal/excessive rates) and 'interest' (legal/normal rates) is often assumed to be a principled economic or ethical distinction; the chapter frames it instead as a specific historical legal compromise (1545 England) that has no basis in the original religious prohibitions, all of which (per the author) opposed any charge for the use of money."],"distinctions":["Original religious prohibition (any interest/Riba forbidden, across Islam, Judaism, Christianity and classical philosophy) vs the later Western legal narrowing (only 'usurious'/above-cap rates prohibited, from 1545 England onward)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","EH.Net Encyclopedia","Distributive justice"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,54]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Capitalism’s view of money capital","b":"Islam’s view of money capital","text":"Capitalism treats it like land and labour, entitled to a return irrespective of outcome; Islam treats it like enterprise, entitled to profit only with risk of loss."}]},{"id":"t3.2.2","section":"3.2.2","title":"Prohibition of Gharar","pages":[57,61],"tier":"core","concepts":["gharar","jahl"],"intuition":"All business involves risk. So what kind of uncertainty makes a contract prohibited? Excessive uncertainty about the subject matter, price or obligations.","simpleExplanation":"Gharar is uncertainty or hazard from lack of clarity about the subject matter or price — like selling a fish in water or a bird in the air. Only excessive Gharar (Gharar-e-Kathir) is prohibited; minor Gharar is tolerated. The goods must exist, be deliverable and be clearly specified, and the price must be known.","academicExplanation":["Gharar means hazard, chance, stake or risk (Khatar). It exists if a party’s liability is uncertain or contingent, delivery is not in any party’s control, or payment is uncertain. Jurists define it as sale of a thing not present, or whose consequence (Aqibah) is unknown — e.g. fish in water, bird in the air. Imam Malik’s examples: a runaway slave, a lost animal, an unborn offspring (though the pregnant cow’s price may reflect it), olives for olive oil.","Uncertainty cannot be avoided and risk-taking is a condition for profit. Scholars distinguish Gharar-e-Kathir (excessive) from Gharar Qalil (nominal); only excessive uncertainty about the subject matter and price is prohibited. Hence Gharar is of less significance than Riba: the slightest Riba invalidates, while some Gharar is acceptable. To prohibit, Gharar must be major, affect principal aspects of the contract, and not be a need of a valid contract such as Salam or Istisna‘a. Ijtihad is still needed in Takaful, secondary markets and derivatives.","Bukhari’s chapter on Gharar covers Habal-al-Hablah (what is in the womb), Mulamasah (sale by touching) and Munabadhah (barter without inspection). Gharar relates to uncertainty about existence, rights/benefits and consequences; some jurists apply it to the doubtful, the Zahiri school (Ibn Hazm) only to the unknown, the majority to both. Al-Dhareer classifies: (I) Gharar in the terms of the contract — two sales in one, ‘Arbun sale, pebble/touch/toss sales, suspended (Mu‘allaq) sale, future sale; (II) Gharar in the object — ignorance of genus, species, attributes, quantity, identity, time of payment in deferred sales; inability to deliver; nonexistent object; not seeing the object.","Islamic law denies the power to sell: things that do not exist; things not in the seller’s possession or not expected to be available; things exchanged with uncertain delivery and payment. Further examples: unspecified item (“one of the houses of this project”), sale of debt without recourse, conditional contracts (“when it rains”), two prices without choosing one, interdependent sales, over-complex contracts, false description, selling at “market price” without specifying, and preventing inspection. Jahl (ignorance about parties, rights, goods or price) is part of Gharar."],"examExplanation":"Gharar = excessive uncertainty/hazard about subject matter, price or obligations (fish in water, bird in air). Only Gharar-e-Kathir prohibited; Gharar Qalil tolerated; less severe than Riba. Tests: major, affects principal aspects, not a need of valid contracts (Salam/Istisna‘a). Avoid: nonexistent, undeliverable, unspecified goods; unknown price/time; two sales in one; Jahl.","keyPoints":["Gharar-e-Kathir (excessive) is prohibited; Gharar Qalil (nominal) is tolerated.","Even slight Riba invalidates; some Gharar is acceptable.","Salam and Istisna‘a meet standards of certainty through conditions.","Jahl (ignorance) is part of Gharar.","Contracts must be free of excessive uncertainty; goods defined, determined and deliverable; quality and quantity stipulated."],"definitions":[{"term":"Gharar","definition":"Uncertainty or hazard caused by lack of clarity regarding the subject matter or price in a contract; excessive Gharar renders a contract prohibited."},{"term":"Gharar-e-Kathir / Gharar Qalil","definition":"Excessive (prohibited) versus nominal (tolerated) uncertainty."},{"term":"Jahl","definition":"Ignorance or non-clarity about the parties, their rights and obligations, the goods or the price — a part of Gharar."},{"term":"Bai‘ al-Ghaib","definition":"Sale of absent or concealed goods; buyer has an option to revoke on sight."}],"examples":[{"title":"Two sales in one (Al-Darir)","kind":"textbook","text":"“I sell you my house at such a price if you sell me your car at such a price” — the first sale depends on the second. Also selling at 100 cash or 110 in a year without choosing — the price is not specified."},{"title":"Two sales in one","kind":"textbook","text":"“I sell you my house at this price if you sell me your car at that price” — neither party knows if the sale will complete, since each is conditional on the other (p. 61)."},{"title":"Sale of the unborn calf","kind":"textbook","text":"The offspring in a cow’s womb cannot be sold, but the pregnant cow may be sold at a higher price because of it (p. 58, n. 32)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Any uncertainty makes a contract void.","right":"Only excessive Gharar affecting principal aspects is prohibited; risk-taking is a condition for profit."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.5","t6.7","t10.4.1","t4.2.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why is Gharar considered of less significance than Riba?","options":["It is not mentioned in Hadith","Some degree of uncertainty is acceptable while the slightest Riba invalidates","It only applies to insurance","It is a Western concept"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Only excessive Gharar is prohibited; any Riba invalidates (p. 58)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.2","sectionTitle":"Prohibition of Gharar","title":"Prohibition of Gharar: Excessive Uncertainty in Contracts","covers":["t3.2.2"],"overview":"The author defines Gharar, distinguishes major/excessive uncertainty (prohibited) from minor/tolerable uncertainty (acceptable), and catalogs the categories and specific examples of Gharar-vitiated transactions.","simpleExplanation":"Gharar means selling something where key facts are unclear or hidden — like selling fish still in the water, or a bird still in the air, or the contents of a sealed box. Total certainty is impossible in any business (some risk-taking is even required to justify earning profit), so scholars only prohibit 'excessive' uncertainty about the core things that matter: what exactly is being sold, whether it can actually be delivered, and what the price is — not minor, unavoidable uncertainty.","academicExplanation":"Gharar refers to uncertainty/hazard from lack of clarity about a contract's subject matter or price; legally, it covers the sale of something not present at hand, something whose 'Aqibah (consequence/outcome) is unknown, or a sale involving genuine hazard about whether the subject will materialize (classic examples: selling fish still in water, or a bird in the air). The author notes literature on Gharar is sparser than on Riba, and that some uncertainty is unavoidable in any business — indeed, risk-bearing is itself a condition for legitimate profit entitlement — so scholars distinguish Gharar-e-Kathir (excessive/major uncertainty, prohibited) from Gharar Qalil (minor/nominal uncertainty, tolerated); a growing consensus focuses the prohibition specifically on transactions where uncertainty is major, remunerative and affects the contract's principal aspects (and is not an inherent feature of an otherwise valid contract type like Salam or Istisna'a, which have their own certainty-ensuring conditions). Imam Malik's definition centers on the sale of an absent object whose good/bad quality is unknown to the buyer (e.g. a runaway slave, a lost animal, an unborn animal still in its mother's womb — though the price of the mother-animal may legitimately rise due to pregnancy — or exchanging olives for olive oil, sesame for sesame oil, butter for butter oil). The Prophet's prohibition of Bai' al Gharar is reported by multiple Companions; Imam Bukhari's chapter heading 'Al Gharar Sale and Habal-al-Hablah' (sale of what is in an animal's womb) is used by jurists (per Al 'Ayny's commentary) to derive the general principle that a contract must not be doubtful/uncertain regarding the parties' rights and obligations — the object, price and terms must be precisely known (Ma'lum). Shaikh Al-Dhareer's classification organizes Gharar into two broad headings: (I) Gharar in the terms/essence of the contract — two sales in one, downpayment ('Arbun) sale, 'pebble'/'touch'/'toss' sales, suspended (Mu'allaq) sale, future sale; (II) Gharar in the object of the contract — ignorance about genus, species, attributes, quantity, specific identity, time of payment in deferred sales, explicit/probable inability to deliver, contracting on a nonexistent object, not seeing the object. To avoid Gharar, Islamic law denies the power to sell: (1) things that don't exist as objects of a legal transaction; (2) things that exist but aren't in the seller's possession or reasonably expected to be available; (3) things exchanged on an uncertain-delivery/payment basis. Abdullah ibn Abbas extended the Gharar prohibition to Bai' al-Ghaib (sale of absent/concealed goods), with the buyer retaining Khiyar (option to revoke) upon actually seeing the goods. Specific illustrative examples given: selling goods the seller cannot deliver (a counterparty/settlement risk — this is precisely why Salam requires the commodity to be reasonably expected available in the market at the stipulated delivery time); conditioning a contract on an unknown future event ('when it rains'); combining two sales in one transaction with ambiguous pricing/conditionality (explicitly prohibited by the Prophet, illustrated by Shaikh Siddiq Al-Darir's example of a conditional house-for-car swap sale); selling based on false description; and various 'value-relevant information unavailable' scenarios (selling milk still in a cow's udder, contents of a sealed box, 'whatever is in my pocket', cloth sold by where a thrown stone lands — Bai' al Hasat — selling at an unspecified 'market price', or denying the buyer proper inspection). Jahl (ignorance about the parties, their rights/obligations, the goods, or the price) is treated as part of Gharar; scholars differ on scope — some jurists (majority) include both 'the unknown' and 'the doubtful' within Gharar, while the Zahiri school (per Ibn Hazm) restricts it to only 'the unknown'. General principles for avoiding Gharar: freedom from excessive subject-matter/price uncertainty; the commodity must be defined, determined and deliverable, with quality/quantity stipulated; no doubt about the parties' rights/obligations; and no Jahl regarding availability, existence and deliverability.","examExplanation":"Gharar = uncertainty/hazard about a contract's subject matter or price. Classic legal examples: selling fish in water, a bird in the air. Only Gharar-e-Kathir (excessive/major uncertainty affecting the contract's principal aspects) is prohibited; Gharar Qalil (minor, unavoidable uncertainty) is tolerated — since total certainty is impossible and risk-bearing is itself required for profit legitimacy. Al-Dhareer's classification: Gharar in the contract's terms (two sales in one, 'Arbun/downpayment sale, pebble/touch/toss sales, suspended sale, future sale) vs Gharar in the object (ignorance of genus/species/attributes/quantity/identity/payment timing, inability to deliver, nonexistent object, unseen object). Three things Islamic law bars selling: nonexistent objects, objects not in the seller's possession/reasonably available, and objects exchanged on uncertain delivery/payment terms. Modern relevance flagged: Takaful, secondary markets for Shari'ah-compliant securities, and derivatives all raise unresolved Gharar/Ijtihad questions.","keyPoints":["Gharar is the sale of a thing not present at hand, or whose consequence/outcome is unknown, or which involves genuine hazard about whether the subject will materialize (classic examples: fish in water, bird in the air).","Only Gharar-e-Kathir (excessive/major uncertainty affecting the contract's principal aspects) is prohibited; Gharar Qalil (minor, unavoidable uncertainty) is tolerated — total certainty is impossible in any business, and risk-bearing is itself a condition for profit entitlement.","Al-Dhareer's classification splits Gharar into: (I) Gharar in the contract's terms/essence (two sales in one, 'Arbun/downpayment sale, pebble/touch/toss sales, suspended sale, future sale), and (II) Gharar in the object of the contract (ignorance of genus, species, attributes, quantity, identity, payment timing; inability to deliver; nonexistent or unseen object).","Islamic law denies the power to sell three categories of things: nonexistent objects, objects not in the seller's possession/reasonably expected available, and objects exchanged on uncertain delivery/payment terms.","Jahl (ignorance about parties, rights/obligations, goods or price) is treated as part of Gharar; the majority of jurists include both 'the unknown' and 'the doubtful' within Gharar, while the Zahiri school restricts it to only 'the unknown'.","The author flags that many areas still require Ijtihad regarding Gharar, particularly Takaful operations, the secondary market for Shari'ah-compliant securities, and possible derivatives in Islamic finance."],"definitions":[{"term":"Gharar","definition":"Uncertainty, hazard or risk relating to major elements of a contract; technically, the sale of a thing not present at hand, or whose consequence/outcome is unknown, or a sale involving an element of chance about whether the subject will materialize."},{"term":"Gharar-e-Kathir / Gharar Qalil","definition":"Excessive/major uncertainty (Kathir — prohibited, as it affects the contract's principal aspects) versus nominal/minor uncertainty (Qalil — tolerated, since total certainty is unattainable in business)."},{"term":"Jahl","definition":"Ignorance or non-clarity about the contracting parties, their rights/obligations, the goods, or the price — treated as a component of Gharar."}],"conditions":["A sale is void if its object does not exist as a legal transaction object, is not in the seller's possession or reasonably expected available, or is exchanged on an uncertain-delivery/payment basis.","For a valid Salam sale, the commodity must reasonably be expected to be available in the market at the stipulated delivery time; selling undeliverable goods is prohibited.","The object of sale must be clearly known (Ma'lum): its genus, species, attributes, quantity and identity must be determined, and the price and payment timing specified."],"principles":["Some uncertainty is unavoidable in any business, and risk-bearing is itself a condition for entitlement to profit — hence only excessive (Kathir), not minor (Qalil), uncertainty is prohibited."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Shaikh Siddiq Al-Darir's conditional house-for-car swap","kind":"textbook","text":"Example of forbidden 'two sales in one': a seller says, 'I sell you my house at such a price if you sell me your car at such a price.' This is Gharar-vitiated because the party selling at one price in cash vs a higher price on credit does not know which price applies, and the house-sale party does not know whether the conditional car-sale will occur — Gharar exists in both the unspecified price and the conditional sale's uncertain occurrence."},{"title":"Bai' al Hasat (pebble sale)","kind":"textbook","text":"Selling a lot of cloth determined by where a thrown stone/pebble lands — an illustrative example of Gharar in the object of the contract, since the specific cloth being sold is undetermined at the time of contracting."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume all uncertainty/risk in a transaction is prohibited; the chapter is explicit that only excessive (Kathir), principal-aspect-affecting uncertainty is prohibited — ordinary business risk (which is in fact required for legitimate profit) is not Gharar."],"distinctions":["Gharar (uncertainty about a contract's core terms — a legal/contractual defect) vs ordinary commercial risk (an economic condition that must exist for profit to be legitimately earned) — the two are conceptually distinct."],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Istisna'a","Khiyar","Maisir/Qimar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,58,59,60,61]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Gharar","b":"Commercial risk","text":"Gharar relates more to uncertainty (existence, rights, consequences) than to the ordinary business risk that entitles one to profit."}],"conditions":["Subject matter must exist (or, as in Salam/Istisna‘a, be defined and deliverable under the contract’s conditions).","Seller must be able to deliver.","Genus, species, attributes, quantity and identity must be known.","Price and time of payment must be specified.","Rights and obligations must not be doubtful.","Buyer must be able to inspect."],"table":{"caption":"Al-Dhareer’s classification of Gharar (pp. 59–60)","head":["Category","Examples"],"rows":[["In the terms of the contract","Two sales in one; ‘Arbun (down-payment) sale; pebble/touch/toss sales; suspended (Mu‘allaq) sale; future sale"],["In the object of the contract","Ignorance of genus, species, attributes, quantity or identity; unknown time of payment; inability to deliver; non-existent object; object not seen"]]}},{"id":"t3.2.3","section":"3.2.3","title":"Prohibition of Maisir/Qimar (Games of Chance)","pages":[61,64],"tier":"core","concepts":["maisir","qimar"],"intuition":"When does a prize, draw or lottery become gambling? When someone gains at another’s cost by chance.","simpleExplanation":"Maisir is getting wealth easily by chance; Qimar is a game of chance where one gains at others’ cost. Gambling is a form of Gharar. Lotteries, draw-based prize bonds, conventional insurance and futures/options settled by price difference involve gambling. Drawing lots to allocate among equals, or a known free gift with a purchase, is permissible.","academicExplanation":["Maisir (from Yusr) means wishing for something valuable with ease, without paying equivalent compensation (‘Iwad), working or undertaking liability, by a game of chance. Qimar is receipt of money or benefit at the cost of others, by chance; it is an important kind of Maisir. The Qur’an uses “Maisir” (2:219; 5:90–91); Hadith literature uses “Qimar”. Gambling is a form of Gharar since the gambler is ignorant of the result.","Pakistan’s Federal Shariat Court held repugnant lotteries in which inducement is an uncertain event and disproportionate prizes are drawn by lots, and schemes where capital is safe but prizes come from interest on the pooled capital. Conventional insurance involves Riba and Maisir; lotteries and draws by governments and corporations, and futures and options settled through price differences, are gambling.","Test for lotteries: in valid lotteries, no one has a vested right and no one is deprived of what he contributed. Drawing lots is permissible to decide among equals who must forgo a right or bear a liability (as with the Prophet Younus), or when a donor/authority selects among equals for a privilege. A known extra product given with a sale is permissible (no chance). Prohibited: causing loss to some so others benefit through lots; disproportionate prizes; coupons leading to draws. Prize bonds distribute precalculated interest by chance, involving both Riba and Maisir, diverting resources from the real sector."],"examExplanation":"Maisir: easy wealth by chance without compensation/work/liability. Qimar: gain at others’ cost by chance (a kind of Maisir). Prohibited: lotteries with disproportionate prizes, prize bonds (Riba + Maisir), conventional insurance (Riba + Maisir), price-difference futures/options. Permitted: drawing lots among equals; known free gift with sale.","keyPoints":["Qur’an: 2:219, 5:90–91.","Gambling is a form of Gharar.","Prize bonds: capital safe but prizes from interest → Riba and Maisir.","Drawing lots among equals is permissible.","Known free product with purchase is permissible.","Futures and options settled only through price differences fall under gambling.","Conventional insurance involves both Riba and Maisir."],"definitions":[{"term":"Maisir","definition":"Wishing for something valuable with ease, without paying equivalent compensation, working or undertaking liability, by way of a game of chance."},{"term":"Qimar","definition":"Receipt of money, benefit or usufruct at the cost of others by resorting to chance — an important kind of Maisir."},{"term":"‘Iwad","definition":"Equivalent compensation/counter-value."}],"examples":[{"title":"Prize bond scheme","kind":"textbook","text":"Investors’ money stays safe, but prizes are the interest generated on the pooled capital, distributed by draw — the winners take interest money by chance at the cost of other bondholders."},{"title":"Permissible draws and incentives","kind":"textbook","text":"Drawing lots is allowed where people of equal standing must be chosen for a right or burden and no one loses what they had (as with Prophet Younus). A free extra product given with a purchase, without any draw, is also permissible (pp. 62–63)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"If the investor’s principal is safe, a prize scheme cannot be gambling.","right":"The FSC held such schemes repugnant because prizes come from interest and are distributed by chance (Riba and Qimar)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.2.1","t8.8.8","t4.2.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A shop gives a known free item with every purchase, without any draw. Is this Maisir?","options":["Yes, it is a game of chance","No — the buyer knows what is purchased and there is no element of chance","Only if the item is valuable","Only for Muslims"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Such incentives are permissible as there is no chance (p. 63)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.2.3","sectionTitle":"Prohibition of Maisir/Qimar (Games of Chance)","title":"Prohibition of Maisir/Qimar: Gambling, Lotteries and Prize Schemes","covers":["t3.2.3"],"overview":"The author defines Maisir and Qimar, gives their Qur'anic basis, explains how gambling is a form of Gharar, and applies the prohibition to modern lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, and price-difference-settled futures/options.","simpleExplanation":"Maisir/Qimar means gambling — getting wealth by pure chance without earning it through work, risk-bearing, or giving something of equal value in return, where one party's gain is exactly another's loss. The Qur'an bans it directly. Modern examples include lotteries, casino-style prize bonds where a lucky few get huge prizes funded by everyone else's forgone interest, conventional insurance (which combines interest and gambling elements), and futures/options contracts settled purely by price differences with no real intention of delivering the underlying asset. Not every drawing of lots is prohibited though — if nobody has a pre-existing claim and no one is deprived of what they already had, drawing lots to fairly allocate something can be legitimate.","academicExplanation":"Maisir and Qimar are used near-interchangeably in Arabic; Qimar is described by jurists as a specific/important subset of Maisir. Maisir (from 'Yusr', ease) means acquiring something valuable with ease, without paying an equivalent compensation ('Iwad), without working for it, or without undertaking any liability against it, via a game of chance; Qimar means receiving money/benefit/usufruct at others' cost, via chance-based entitlement. The Qur'an uses 'Maisir' for the prohibition (verses 2:219, 5:90-91); Hadith literature generally discusses it as 'Qimar'. Key verses: 5:90 (intoxicants and gambling are 'abominable actions of Satan' to be abstained from); 5:91 (Satan uses intoxicants/gambling to excite enmity/hatred and to hinder remembrance of Allah/prayer); 4:219 (wine and gambling contain 'great sin and some benefits... but the sin is greater than the benefits'). Gambling is characterized as a form of Gharar, since the gambler is ignorant of the gamble's result. Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court (FSC) rulings are cited: a lottery inducing participation via an uncertain/unknown chance-based event, or distributing disproportionate prizes by drawing lots, is repugnant to Shari'ah; a scheme where investors' principal is safe/intact but prizes are funded from interest generated on pooled capital is also repugnant, due to Qimar. The author gives a test distinguishing valid from invalid 'lottery'-type mechanisms: in valid cases, no participant has any personal/vested right at stake and no one is deprived of something they already possessed or contributed — drawing lots is then simply a neutral, fair allocation method among equals (e.g. Prophet Younus's case, or a grantor with equal-footing candidates choosing by lot); similarly, promotional giveaways where a purchaser knowingly buys a known product at a known price, with an extra incentive item given (no drawing/chance involved), are permissible, since there's no element of chance in what was actually purchased. However, where the mechanism causes loss to some participants for others to gain (disproportionate prizes via drawing of lots, chance-based inducements via tickets/coupons/tabs), it is prohibited — the author notes such schemes often combine Riba, Gharar, Jahl and Qimar simultaneously, since the pooled proceeds function as debt and the 'prize' is really a redistributed portion of predetermined interest payments from the bank (borrower) to lenders, while exaggerated publicity (implying near-certain winning odds when true odds are one in hundreds of thousands) constitutes additional deception. Regarding financial institutions specifically: prize bond schemes (investors' principal safe, but prizes funded from interest on pooled capital, distributed by chance to a few at others' expense) are ruled repugnant due to both Riba and Maisir; schemes giving most participants a meagre/no return while diverting the difference to a few large 'prizes' cause loss to the majority for the benefit of a few, diverting resources from real economic activity into speculation. The chapter also flags related modern applications: conventional insurance is deemed non-Shari'ah-compliant due to both Riba and Maisir involvement; government/corporate resource mobilization via lottery/draws is prohibited as gambling; futures and options contracts settled purely through price differences (without genuine delivery intent) fall under the gambling prohibition.","examExplanation":"Maisir = acquiring wealth easily/by chance without equivalent compensation, work, or liability; Qimar = a subset of Maisir, receiving money/benefit at another's cost via chance. Qur'anic basis: 2:219, 5:90-91 (gambling grouped with intoxicants as 'abominable Satanic actions'). Gambling is a form of Gharar (the gambler doesn't know the outcome). Test for valid vs invalid lot-drawing: valid if no participant has a pre-existing vested right and no one is deprived of something they already had/contributed (neutral fair allocation among equals); invalid if it causes loss to some for others' disproportionate gain (typical prize-bond/lottery structure). Modern applications ruled prohibited: government/corporate lotteries and draws; bank prize-bond schemes (funded from pooled-capital interest, combining Riba + Maisir); conventional insurance (Riba + Maisir); futures/options settled purely by price differences without genuine delivery intent.","keyPoints":["Maisir = easily acquired wealth/chance-based gain without equivalent compensation, work, or liability; Qimar = a specific, important subset of Maisir involving gain at another's direct cost via chance.","Qur'anic basis: verses 2:219, 5:90, 5:91 — gambling is grouped with intoxicants as an 'abominable action of Satan' whose sin outweighs any benefit.","Gambling is classified as a form of Gharar, since the gambler is ignorant of the gamble's outcome.","Test for permissible lot-drawing: valid when no participant has a pre-existing vested right and no one is deprived of something already possessed/contributed (neutral, fair allocation); invalid when it causes loss to some participants for others' disproportionate gain.","A promotional giveaway (known product, known price, bonus item with no drawing/chance involved) is permissible — there is no element of chance in what is actually purchased.","Modern applications ruled prohibited due to Maisir/Qimar (often combined with Riba and Gharar): government/corporate lotteries and draws, bank prize-bond schemes, conventional insurance, and futures/options contracts settled purely by price differences without genuine delivery intent."],"definitions":[{"term":"Maisir","definition":"Acquisition of wealth by chance/easily, without paying an equivalent compensation ('Iwad) for it, without working for it, or without undertaking any liability against it."},{"term":"Qimar","definition":"Games of chance/gambling; an arrangement in which possession of property is contingent on the happening of an uncertain event, such that one party gains and another loses, without specifying in advance which party will gain and which will lose."}],"conditions":["A lot-drawing scheme is permissible only if no participant has a pre-existing personal/vested right at stake and no one is deprived of what they already possessed or contributed."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Bank prize bond schemes","kind":"textbook","text":"Investors' principal remains safe/intact, but the prizes are funded from interest generated on the pooled capital and distributed by chance to a small number of bondholders at the expense of the rest — ruled repugnant to Shari'ah due to the combined involvement of Riba and Maisir."}],"commonConfusions":["Not all drawing of lots is gambling — students should distinguish neutral, fair-allocation lot-drawing (permissible, e.g. among equally entitled candidates) from chance-based schemes that create winners and losers out of pooled stakes (prohibited)."],"distinctions":["Permissible promotional incentive (known product/price, bonus given, no chance element in the purchase itself) vs prohibited lottery/prize scheme (chance-based winner selection causing loss to non-winning participants)."],"relatedConcepts":["Gharar","Riba","Takaful","Conventional insurance"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,62,63,64]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Permissible drawing of lots","b":"Prohibited lottery","text":"Lots may allocate among equals where nobody loses what they contributed; prohibited lotteries make some lose so others gain disproportionate prizes by chance."}]},{"id":"t3.3","section":"3.3","title":"Business Ethics and Norms","pages":[64,64],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["business-ethics"],"intuition":"Beyond the three prohibitions, what behaviour does Shari’ah expect in business?","simpleExplanation":"Shari’ah sets norms for business: justice, mutual help, free consent, honesty, avoiding fraud and misrepresentation. Because people are accountable to Allah, and harm to others is forgiven only by those harmed, giving everyone their due is the cardinal principle.","academicExplanation":["Principles of justice, mutual help, free consent and honesty, avoiding fraud, misrepresentation and misstatement, and negation of exploitation provide grounds for valid contracts. These norms relate to accountability before Allah and thus differ in implication from mainstream business ethics: Allah may pardon faults against His rights but not harm done to fellow beings. Encouragements include benevolence, purification of income, transparency and disclosure, documentation of rights and liabilities, and care for others."],"examExplanation":"Norms: justice, mutual help, free consent, honesty; no fraud/misrepresentation. Cardinal principle: give people their due (harm to others not pardoned unless they forgive). Encouragements: benevolence, purification, transparency, documentation.","keyPoints":["Giving people their due right is the cardinal principle of Islamic ethics.","Encouraged practices: benevolence, purification of income, transparency and disclosure, and documenting transactions.","Allah may pardon faults against His rights but not harm done to fellow beings — giving people their due is the cardinal ethical principle."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Rights of Allah and of people","kind":"practice","text":"A trader who misses a voluntary fast may seek Allah’s forgiveness; a trader who cheated a customer must also make it right with that customer, because harm to fellow beings is forgiven only by those harmed (p. 64)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.3.1","t3.3.2","t3.3.3","t3.3.4","t3.3.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What is the cardinal principle of the Islamic system of ethics according to the chapter?","options":["Profit maximisation","Giving people their due right","Minimising taxes","Avoiding contracts"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 64."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t3.3.1","section":"3.3.1","title":"Justice and Fair Dealing (Honesty, Najash, Khalabah, Disclosure)","pages":[64,67],"tier":"core","concepts":["business-ethics","najash","late-payment-penalty"],"intuition":"What does “fair dealing” mean in practice for a trader or a bank?","simpleExplanation":"Be just even to enemies; don’t take others’ property wrongfully; be honest and disclose defects; don’t bid up prices without intent to buy (Najash); don’t mislead customers (Khalabah); let buyers inspect goods and have market information. Banks may fine wilful defaulters, with the fine going to charity.","academicExplanation":["Justice — fair dealing and balance — is the foremost principle (5:8; 4:135; 2:188). Rights and obligations are equal for all; the Prophet’s last sermon declared the inviolability of person, property and honour.","Honesty and gentleness: the truthful merchant will be with the Prophets; a defect must be disclosed. The Prophet sought refuge from debt because a debtor may lie and break promises — loans are not prohibited but should be taken only in genuine need. “No deception” — deception gives a right to rescind. Ghaban (defrauding in specifications or price) is prohibited. Gentleness in buying, selling and demanding rights is praised; the Prophet repaid a camel with a better one: “the best among you are those who repay their debts handsomely.” Institutions managing public money differ from individuals: Islamic banks’ Shari’ah boards allow fines on wilful defaulters as a deterrent, used for charity and not credited to the bank’s P&L (“procrastination by a wealthy person is injustice”).","Najash — bidding up a price without intending to buy — is not permissible. Khalabah — misleading marketing, overprojecting quality, excessive swearing — is prohibited, including misleading advertising. Disclosure and transparency: deceiving a Mustarsal (unknowing entrant) is Riba; concealing vital information gives the disadvantaged party the right to rescind; the wet-grain Hadith: “He who deceives is not one of us.” Meeting grain dealers (Rukban) before they reach the market is prohibited, so prices form with full information. Withholding value-related information amounts to Gharar and Jahl; information should be equally accessible to all investors."],"examExplanation":"Justice foremost; honesty and disclosure of defects; Ghaban prohibited; gentleness; fines on wilful defaulters go to charity. Najash (bidding up without intent) and Khalabah (misleading marketing) prohibited. Transparency: inspection, market information, no meeting Rukban, info equally accessible.","keyPoints":["Defects must be pointed out.","Deception allows rescission of the contract.","Institutional fines on wilful defaulters are used for charity, not income.","Najash: “a Najish is a cursed taker of Riba.”","Deceiving a Mustarsal is Riba.","Value-related information should be equally accessible to all investors."],"definitions":[{"term":"Najash","definition":"Bidding up the price without intention to take delivery of the commodity — prohibited."},{"term":"Khalabah","definition":"Misleading marketing, e.g. overprojecting quality to unaware clients — prohibited."},{"term":"Ghaban","definition":"Misappropriation or defrauding others regarding specifications of goods or prices."},{"term":"Mustarsal","definition":"An unknowing entrant into the market; deceiving him is described as Riba."}],"examples":[{"title":"The wet grain","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) put his hand in a heap of grain and found it wet inside: “He who deceives other people is not one of us” (p. 67)."},{"title":"Do not meet the caravans (Rukban)","kind":"textbook","text":"Buyers were told not to intercept traders before they reached the market, so prices would be set by demand and supply with full information (p. 67)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Because Islam encourages leniency with debtors, Islamic banks cannot take any action against defaulters.","right":"Banks manage public money as a trust; Shari’ah boards allow fines on wilful defaulters as a deterrent, with proceeds given to charity."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.13","t9.9.4","t17.4.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"An agent bids in an auction only to push up the price, with no intention to buy. This is:","options":["Khalabah","Najash","Ghaban","Tawarruq"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Najash (p. 66)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.3.1","sectionTitle":"Business Ethics and Norms — Justice and Fair Dealing","title":"Business Ethics I: Justice, Honesty, and the Prohibitions of Najash, Khalabah and Non-Disclosure","covers":["t3.3.1"],"overview":"Beyond the three major prohibitions, the author details the positive ethical framework governing Islamic business: justice/fair dealing, honesty and gentleness, and specific prohibitions on price manipulation (Najash), misleading marketing (Khalabah), and non-disclosure.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic business ethics requires being just and honest with everyone, even people you dislike — no one's rights are worth more or less than anyone else's. A truthful, honest merchant is promised great spiritual reward. Specific bad practices are banned by name: Najash (fake bidding to artificially drive up a price without intending to actually buy), Khalabah (misleading marketing/exaggerated claims about a product), and hiding defects or relevant facts from a buyer. Sellers must let buyers properly inspect goods, and both buyers and sellers should have equal access to market information.","academicExplanation":"Justice (fair dealing, maintaining balance) is presented as the foremost principle governing all economic activity, quoting Qur'an 5:8 ('let not the enmity and hatred of others make you avoid justice; be just, that is nearest to piety') and 4:135 (believers must stand as witnesses for fair play) — obligating justice even toward enemies — and 2:188 (prohibiting unjust appropriation of others' property, including bribery of rulers). The Prophet's Farewell Pilgrimage sermon declared the inviolability of persons, property and honour for all. Honesty/gentleness norms: 'the truthful and honest merchant shall be with the Prophets, the truthful and the martyrs on the Day of Resurrection'; sellers must disclose known defects ('it is not lawful for a Muslim to sell to his brother something defective without pointing out the defect'); deliberate deception and lying are grave sins; a buyer facing frequent unfair bargains was advised by the Prophet to say 'No deception' at the point of sale, giving a right to rescind on discovered deception; Ghaban (misappropriation/defrauding regarding goods' specifications and price) is prohibited to ensure sellers deliver goods as represented and charge fair prices (Qur'an 17:35 on giving full measure and weighing justly). Gentleness is commended even in demanding one's rights ('May Allah have mercy on a person who is gentle when he sells, when he buys, and when he demands his rights') — illustrated by the Prophet's own conduct toward an aggressive creditor, instructing his Companions to repay a debt with an even better animal than owed, since 'the best among you are those who repay their debts handsomely.' The author distinguishes individual-to-individual leniency (praiseworthy, e.g. waiving a genuinely destitute debtor's obligation) from institutional context (banks managing public funds as a trust cannot extend blanket leniency to wilful defaulters — Shari'ah boards permit penalty fines on wilful defaulters as a deterrent, provided the fine proceeds go to charity, not the bank's P&L, detailed further in Chapter 7); 'procrastination in repaying debts by a wealthy person is injustice' (Abu Huraira). Najash (bidding up a price with no intention to purchase, to manipulate other buyers) is explicitly cursed ('a Najish is a cursed taker of Riba'), and market price manipulation generally is condemned ('if anyone interferes in the market to create a rise in prices, God has right to cast him face down in Hell'). Khalabah (misleading marketing — overprojecting a commodity's quality to unaware/simple clients) is prohibited as unethical deception; excessive swearing/oath-taking while selling is discouraged ('it may increase business... but brings destruction ultimately') — covering modern misleading advertising. Disclosure/transparency/inspection norms: buyers must be given ample opportunity to inspect goods; concealing defects is dishonest (illustrated by the Prophet discovering wet grain hidden beneath a dry surface layer and declaring 'he who deceives other people is not one of us'); 'deceiving a Mustarsal' (an unknowing market entrant) 'is Riba' — equating severe deception with the gravity of Riba itself; informationally disadvantaged parties may rescind a contract. The Prophet also prohibited intercepting rural traders (Rukban) before they reached town markets to buy from them at an information disadvantage, or engaging in Najash to excite competitive buying — establishing that all market parties should transact with adequate, symmetric information about quality, value, and demand/supply conditions, ideally within the open market itself where such information is available to all.","examExplanation":"Justice (fair dealing, applying even to enemies — Qur'an 5:8, 4:135) is the foremost business-ethics principle. Named prohibitions: Najash (fake/shill bidding to inflate price without buying intent — 'a Najish is a cursed taker of Riba'); Khalabah (misleading marketing/overprojected quality claims); non-disclosure of known defects; deceiving a Mustarsal (unknowing market entrant) is equated in severity to Riba. Positive norms: honesty (truthful merchants ranked with Prophets/martyrs); gentleness even when demanding rights; full disclosure and buyer's right to inspect goods; symmetric market information for all parties (illustrated by the prohibition on intercepting rural traders before they reach the open market).","keyPoints":["Justice is the foremost principle governing Islamic economic activity, required even toward enemies (Qur'an 5:8, 4:135); the Prophet's Farewell Sermon declared the inviolability of person, property and honour for all.","A truthful, honest merchant is promised to be ranked with the Prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs on the Day of Resurrection; sellers must disclose known defects in what they sell.","Najash (fake bidding to inflate a price without intent to buy, manipulating other buyers) is explicitly prohibited and its practitioner cursed as 'a cursed taker of Riba'.","Khalabah (misleading marketing — overprojecting a commodity's quality) is prohibited as deception; excessive oath-swearing while selling is discouraged for the same reason.","Deceiving a Mustarsal (an unknowing/naive market entrant) is described as being equivalent in severity to Riba — an unusually strong equation used to stress the gravity of exploiting an information-disadvantaged buyer.","Banks (managing public funds as a trust) may impose penalty fines on wilful defaulters as a deterrent, with proceeds directed to charity, not the bank's own profit — distinct from the individual-level virtue of waiving a genuinely destitute debtor's obligation."],"definitions":[{"term":"Najash","definition":"Bidding up the price of a commodity without any intention of purchasing or taking delivery of it, done to manipulate other genuine buyers into paying more."},{"term":"Khalabah","definition":"Misleading marketing — pursuing unaware or simple clients by overprojecting the quality of a commodity beyond its actual characteristics."},{"term":"Ghaban","definition":"Misappropriation or defrauding others in respect of the specifications of goods and their prices."},{"term":"Mustarsal","definition":"An unknowing entrant into the market, unfamiliar with prevailing prices/practices — deceiving such a person is described in Hadith as equivalent to Riba."}],"conditions":["A buyer must be given ample opportunity to see and inspect a commodity before purchase; concealing known defects or relevant information vitiates fair dealing and gives the disadvantaged party the right to rescind."],"principles":["Giving people their due right is the cardinal principle of the Islamic system of ethics — Allah may pardon lapses regarding His own rights, but not harm done to fellow human beings."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet and the wet grain","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet, passing a grain seller, put his hand into the heap and found it wet underneath a dry top layer (concealing lower quality). He declared: 'He who deceives other people is not one of us' — a foundational precedent against concealment of defects."},{"title":"Repaying a debt 'handsomely'","kind":"textbook","text":"A creditor demanded his debt (a camel) from the Prophet rudely; the Prophet instructed his Companions to buy and give the creditor an even better camel than owed, saying 'the best among you are those who repay their debts handsomely' — illustrating gentleness even toward an aggressive creditor and generosity in debt repayment."}],"commonConfusions":["The permissibility of banks fining wilful defaulters (for deterrence, proceeds to charity) should not be confused with charging interest on overdue debt — the fine is a disciplinary/charitable mechanism, not compensation to the bank, and is distinct from Riba."],"distinctions":["Individual-to-individual leniency toward a genuinely destitute debtor (praiseworthy, per Qur'an) vs institutional (bank) treatment of a wilful defaulter (penalty fines permitted as deterrence, proceeds to charity, not the bank's income)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Gharar","Ghaban-e-Fahish","Khiyar"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,65,66,67]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Individual creditor","b":"Bank as creditor","text":"An individual may waive a genuinely troubled debtor’s debt as a virtue; a bank managing public money as a trust may impose fines on wilful defaulters, with the fines going to charity (p. 66)."}]},{"id":"t3.3.2","section":"3.3.2","title":"Fulfilling the Covenants and Paying Liabilities","pages":[67,68],"tier":"core","concepts":["wad"],"intuition":"Is a promise in business just a moral matter, or can it have financial consequences? In Islamic finance, promises are relied on — and breach can cost you.","simpleExplanation":"Muslims must fulfil contracts and even promises. Contemporary scholars consider promises binding; they are used in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, leasing and Diminishing Musharakah. If the promisor breaks the promise, the promisee can recover actual loss.","academicExplanation":["“And keep the covenant. Lo! Of the covenant it will be asked” (17:34). Contracts create rights and liabilities to be fulfilled; Shari’ah also emphasises fulfilling promises or unilateral undertakings — breaking promises is a sign of hypocrites. Contemporary scholars unanimously consider promises binding. The concept is invoked in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, leasing and Diminishing Musharakah; if the promisor does not fulfil the promise, the promisee can recover the actual loss incurred."],"examExplanation":"Fulfil contracts and promises (17:34). Contemporary scholars: promises binding; used in MPO, Ijarah, Diminishing Musharakah; breach → recovery of actual loss.","keyPoints":["Promises (Wa‘d) binding per contemporary scholars.","Remedy for breach: actual loss, not a penalty.","Verse 17:34: “Keep the covenant — of the covenant it will be asked.”"],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Broken promise in MPO","kind":"textbook","text":"A client promises to buy a car the bank has bought for him, then refuses. The bank sells the car in the market; it can recover only the actual loss from the client, not a penalty or its cost of funds (pp. 67–68; see Section 5.6)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A promise in Islamic finance is only morally binding and has no financial consequence.","right":"Contemporary scholars regard promises as binding; in MPO, leasing and Diminishing Musharakah the promisee may recover the actual loss caused by a breach (pp. 67–68)."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.6","t9.8.2","t12.9.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If a customer breaches a binding promise to buy in a Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, what can the bank recover?","options":["Any penalty it chooses","The actual loss incurred","Interest for the period","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The promisee may recover the actual loss (p. 68)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch3-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch3-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"3.3.2","sectionTitle":"Business Ethics and Norms — Covenants, Cooperation, Pricing and Dharar","title":"Business Ethics II: Covenants, Mutual Cooperation, Fair Pricing and Freedom from Dharar","covers":["t3.3.2"],"overview":"The author completes the business-ethics framework: the binding nature of promises and covenants, Islam's pre-Islamic-derived tradition of mutual-help risk-pooling (the root of Takaful), the rules of free-market pricing with limited State intervention, and the principle of protecting third parties from contractual harm (Dharar).","simpleExplanation":"Muslims must keep their promises and honor their agreements — breaking promises is a mark of hypocrisy, and modern scholars agree unilateral promises (used in products like Murabaha and leasing) are binding, with the wronged party entitled to recover actual losses if a promise is broken. Islam also encourages mutual help — historically, tribes pooled funds to cover disaster losses or trade-route risks together, a tradition that is the direct ancestor of modern Takaful (Islamic insurance). On pricing, Islam wants free markets where prices reflect real supply and demand — you can't undercut the market out of misplaced generosity (that hurts other sellers long-term), and you can't manipulate or hoard to inflate prices either; the government should generally stay out of price-setting, stepping in only to fix genuine market abuses. Finally, if a private deal between two people would unfairly harm a third party, that third party gets legal protections too.","academicExplanation":"Fulfilling covenants (Qur'an 17:34, from the twelve commandments in Surah Bani Israel: 'keep the covenant, for the covenant will be asked about') extends beyond formal contracts to promises/unilateral undertakings — contemporary scholars unanimously treat promises as binding, a principle invoked in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, leasing, and Diminishing Musharakah; if a promisor reneges, the promisee may recover their actual loss from the breach (detailed further in Chapter 5). Mutual cooperation and hardship removal (Qur'an 5:2: 'assist one another in goodness and righteousness, not in sin and transgression'; the Prophet's 'one body' Hadith on believers' mutual mercy) is illustrated via pre-Islamic customs validated by Islam: 'Aqilah (kin-based shared responsibility, e.g. collective contribution during calamity or for blood-money payment, distributing burden across a tribe) and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (jointly pooled funds indemnifying traders' journey losses from route hazards) — explicitly identified as the historical foundation of the modern institution of Takaful. Free marketing and fair pricing: Islam permits Halal business freedom but not unlimited contracting freedom — exchange must follow Shari'ah rules for the relevant contract type (Bai', Ijarah, services). Fair prices are those emerging from genuinely free market forces (supply and demand) without interference; Ghaban-e-Fahish (selling at an inflated price while implying it is the market rate) is prohibited. Selling below cost out of misguided philanthropy is also discouraged, since it distorts future supply and harms other legitimate sellers — illustrated by Caliph Umar's historical intervention requiring a below-market seller to raise their price or exit the market. However, the State/regulators must intervene against vested interests obstructing market forces or hoarding to create artificial scarcity — intervention is conditioned on removing genuine market anomalies caused by impaired competition, not routine price-setting. The Jeddah-based Council of the Islamic Fiqh Academy of the OIC (5th session, 1988) laid down four operative principles: (1) freedom to buy/sell/dispose of property within Shari'ah limits (Qur'an 4:29 on trade by mutual consent) is the basic rule; (2) no fixed restriction on the percentage profit a trader may earn — this is left to merchants, the business environment and the nature of goods, subject to Shari'ah-recommended ethics (moderation, contentment, leniency); (3) transactions must avoid fraud, cheating, deceit, forgery and concealment harmful to society/individuals; (4) government should not fix prices except when clear market pitfalls/artificial price distortions are identified, in which case it should intervene specifically to remove those causal factors (fraud, artificial scarcity), not to override genuine market pricing generally. Freedom from Dharar (detriment/harm) protects third parties from harm caused by a contract between two other consenting parties. The State/regulators must ensure fair play and prevent vested interests from creating hardship for the masses; where investors are found broadly naive/irrational, regulators may take a protective/paternalistic approach (providing necessary information, guarding against irrational reactions to disinformation). A concrete legal mechanism illustrating third-party protection is Shuf'ah (the pre-emptive right of a partner in joint ownership), which the author notes could by analogy extend to protecting minority shareholders in joint-stock companies from being adversely affected by controlling shareholders' unilateral decisions (e.g. dilutive share issuances, management changes, mergers/acquisitions).","examExplanation":"Promises/unilateral undertakings are binding by scholarly consensus (relevant to Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, leasing, Diminishing Musharakah); a broken promise entitles the promisee to recover their actual loss. 'Aqilah and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (pre-Islamic mutual-risk-pooling customs validated by Islam) are the direct historical foundation of Takaful. Fair pricing = genuine, unmanipulated supply-and-demand outcomes; Ghaban-e-Fahish (charging inflated prices while implying market rates) is prohibited; selling below cost out of misplaced philanthropy is also discouraged (Caliph Umar precedent). The OIC Fiqh Academy's 1988 ruling: no fixed cap on trader profit margins; government intervenes in pricing only to remove genuine market distortions (fraud, hoarding), not routinely. Freedom from Dharar protects third parties from harm caused by others' contracts — illustrated by Shuf'ah (pre-emptive right), analogized to protecting minority shareholders from controlling shareholders' harmful decisions.","keyPoints":["Fulfilling covenants extends to unilateral promises, not just formal bilateral contracts (Qur'an 17:34); contemporary scholars unanimously treat promises as binding — directly relevant to Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, leasing, and Diminishing Musharakah.","'Aqilah (tribal shared-responsibility for calamity/blood-money) and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (pooled indemnification of trade-route losses) are pre-Islamic customs validated by Islam, and are identified as the direct historical foundation of the modern Takaful institution.","Fair prices must emerge from genuinely free market forces; Ghaban-e-Fahish (charging an inflated price while implying it is the market rate) is prohibited, and selling below cost out of misplaced philanthropy is also discouraged since it distorts future supply (Caliph Umar precedent).","The OIC Fiqh Academy (1988) ruled there is no fixed restriction on a trader's profit percentage (left to the merchant, market and goods, subject to Shari'ah ethics), and that government should not fix prices except to remove clearly identified market distortions/fraud.","Freedom from Dharar protects third parties from harm caused by a contract between two other consenting parties; Shuf'ah (a partner's pre-emptive right in joint ownership) illustrates this, and the author suggests it could analogously protect minority shareholders from harmful decisions by controlling shareholders."],"definitions":[{"term":"'Aqilah","definition":"A pre-Islamic tribal custom of shared responsibility/mutual help, whereby kin/tribal members jointly contributed to relieve a calamity or to pay blood-money on behalf of a member — validated by Islam and a historical root of Takaful."},{"term":"Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq","definition":"An arrangement of mutual assistance in which losses suffered by traders during journeys due to hazards on trade routes were indemnified from jointly pooled funds."},{"term":"Ghaban-e-Fahish","definition":"Excessive profiteering with deception — selling a commodity while stating or implying that the market price is being charged, when in fact an exorbitant price is being charged."},{"term":"Shuf'ah","definition":"The pre-emptive right of a partner in joint ownership (or a neighbour, in the sale of landed property)."}],"conditions":["Government price intervention is warranted only to remove identified market distortions (fraud, hoarding, impaired competition) — not as routine price-setting overriding genuine supply-and-demand outcomes."],"principles":["There is no fixed Shari'ah-mandated cap on a trader's profit margin — this is left to the merchant, the business environment, and the nature of the goods, subject to the ethics of moderation, contentment and leniency."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Caliph Umar and the below-market seller","kind":"textbook","text":"Caliph Umar (the second Caliph) required a trader selling below the market price to raise the price to the market level or leave the market — illustrating that even well-intentioned underpricing can be restricted because it disrupts fair market functioning for other genuine sellers over time."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes assume Islamic economics caps profit margins for 'fairness'; the OIC Fiqh Academy ruling explicitly states there is no fixed restriction on profit percentage — fairness is ensured through process (free market, no fraud/manipulation), not a mandated margin ceiling."],"distinctions":["Permissible State intervention (removing identified market distortions/fraud/hoarding) vs impermissible State intervention (routine price-fixing overriding genuine market forces)."],"relatedConcepts":["Takaful","Murabaha","Diminishing Musharakah","Al-Hisbah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[67,68,69,70]}}]},{"id":"t3.3.3","section":"3.3.3","title":"Mutual Cooperation and Removal of Hardship","pages":[68,68],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["takaful","aqilah"],"intuition":"Where does the idea behind Takaful come from? From pre-Islamic mutual-help schemes that Islam endorsed.","simpleExplanation":"Islam encourages helping one another (5:2). Pre-Islamic mutual schemes — ‘Aqilah (tribe sharing blood money and calamities) and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (joint funds compensating traders’ losses on routes) — were validated. This principle of reciprocal compensation is the foundation of Takaful.","academicExplanation":["Mutual help, solidarity and joint indemnification are important norms, in contrast with cut-throat competition that encourages fraud. “Assist one another in goodness … not in sin” (5:2); believers are like one body. ‘Aqilah worked on shared responsibility — contributions in calamities and tribal payment of blood money. Under Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq, traders’ losses from route hazards were indemnified from jointly created funds. Islam accepted reciprocal compensation and joint responsibility — the foundation of Takaful."],"examExplanation":"Mutual cooperation (5:2). ‘Aqilah and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq validated by Islam → principle of reciprocal compensation and joint responsibility → foundation of Takaful.","keyPoints":["‘Aqilah: shared tribal responsibility.","Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq: joint fund for route losses.","Foundation of Takaful."],"definitions":[{"term":"‘Aqilah","definition":"Pre-Islamic custom of shared responsibility among kin — contributing in calamities and paying blood money jointly."},{"term":"Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq","definition":"Indemnifying traders’ losses from hazards on trade routes from jointly created funds."}],"examples":[{"title":"Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq","kind":"textbook","text":"Traders pooled money to compensate any of them who lost goods to hazards on trade routes — a pre-Islamic scheme of joint indemnity that Islam accepted and that underlies Takaful (p. 68)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islam rejects all pre-Islamic customs.","right":"Islam validated beneficial practices such as ‘Aqilah and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq — the basis of Takaful (p. 68)."}],"relatedTopics":["t16.3","t16.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which principle does the author identify as the foundation of Takaful?","options":["Riba Al-Fadl","Reciprocal compensation and joint responsibility","Najash","Hawalah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 68."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t3.3.4","section":"3.3.4","title":"Free Marketing and Fair Pricing","pages":[68,69],"tier":"core","concepts":["fair-pricing","hisbah"],"intuition":"Is there a cap on profit margins in Islam? No — but prices must come from genuine market forces, not manipulation.","simpleExplanation":"Islam allows any Halal business under Shari’ah rules. Fair prices come from free demand and supply. There is no limit on profit percentage, but traders should be moderate. Ghaban-e-Fahish (overcharging while pretending it is the market rate) is prohibited. The State intervenes only to remove market distortions like hoarding.","academicExplanation":["Freedom to enter Halal business is not unbridled; exchange must be in permissible commodities according to the rules of Bai‘, Ijarah and services. Fair prices result from genuinely free market forces. Ghaban-e-Fahish — selling at a higher price while implying it is the market rate — is prohibited. Price reflects input, production, storage, transport costs and profit; selling below cost out of piety can harm supply — hence Umar asked such a trader to raise his rate or leave. If vested interests hoard or manipulate, the State must intervene, but only to remove anomalies impairing free competition.","The OIC Islamic Fiqh Academy (5th session, 1988): (1) freedom to buy, sell and dispose of property within Shari’ah (trade by mutual consent); (2) no restriction on the percentage of profit, subject to ethics of moderation, contentment and leniency; (3) keep transactions away from fraud, cheating, deceit, forgery and concealment; (4) government should fix prices only when obvious pitfalls and artificial factors are noticed."],"examExplanation":"Fair price = outcome of genuine market forces. No cap on profit % (Fiqh Academy 1988), but moderation. Ghaban-e-Fahish prohibited. State intervenes only to remove distortions (hoarding, manipulation).","keyPoints":["No restriction on percentage of profit (Fiqh Academy, 1988).","Ghaban-e-Fahish prohibited.","Price fixing only to correct artificial distortions."],"definitions":[{"term":"Ghaban-e-Fahish","definition":"Selling at a higher price while giving the impression that the client is charged the market rate."}],"examples":[{"title":"Umar and the under-pricing trader","kind":"textbook","text":"A trader selling below cost out of piety can disrupt supply for others, so Umar asked him to raise his price to the market level or leave the market (pp. 68–69)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic banks must limit their profit margin to a fixed percentage.","right":"The Fiqh Academy states there is no restriction on the percentage of profit; ethics of moderation apply."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.4","t6.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What did the OIC Fiqh Academy (1988) say about the percentage of profit a trader may make?","options":["Maximum 10%","No restriction, subject to Shari’ah ethics like moderation","Must equal the interest rate","Must be approved by the State"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 69."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t3.3.5","section":"3.3.5","title":"Freedom from Dharar (Detriment)","pages":[69,70],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["dharar","shufah"],"intuition":"Can a contract between two willing parties still be wrong? Yes, if it harms a third party.","simpleExplanation":"Contracts must not harm others. Regulators may protect naïve investors and provide information. A third party harmed by a contract may have rights — e.g. a partner’s pre-emptive right (Shuf‘ah), which may be extended by analogy to minority shareholders.","academicExplanation":["Dharar refers to saving others from harm due to a contract between two parties. The informationally disadvantaged party has an option to reverse its position. The State must ensure fair play; if most investors are naïve or irrational, regulators may take a paternal approach and provide information. If a contract is detrimental to a third party, the latter may have rights — e.g. Shuf‘ah (pre-emptive right) of a partner in joint ownership, which may be extended by analogy to minority shareholders affected by controlling shareholders’ decisions (new issues, management change, mergers)."],"examExplanation":"Dharar: contracts must not harm third parties. Disadvantaged party may rescind; regulators may protect naïve investors. Shuf‘ah (pre-emption) may extend by analogy to minority shareholders.","keyPoints":["Paternal regulatory approach may be justified.","Shuf‘ah as an example of third-party rights.","Regulators may take a paternal approach to protect naïve investors and must provide the public with information."],"definitions":[{"term":"Dharar","definition":"Detriment/harm — contracts should not harm others."},{"term":"Shuf‘ah","definition":"Pre-emptive right of a partner in joint ownership."}],"examples":[{"title":"Shuf‘ah extended to minority shareholders","kind":"textbook","text":"The pre-emptive right of a co-owner may be extended by analogy to protect minority shareholders harmed by controlling shareholders’ decisions — new share issues, management change, mergers (p. 70)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.3.1","t12.2.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which right is cited as an example of protecting a third party from a contract’s detriment?","options":["Shuf‘ah (pre-emption)","Hawalah","‘Arbun","Ju‘alah"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"p. 70."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]}],"questions":[{"id":"q3.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which verse is described as the first express prohibition of Riba for Muslims?","options":["30:39","4:161","3:130","2:275"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"3:130, around the battle of Uhad (p. 44).","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the textual basis of Riba prohibition","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.2","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the author, charging a ‘reasonable’ rate of interest on a productive commercial loan is not Riba.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Any addition over the principal is Riba irrespective of rate or purpose.","topicId":"t3.2.1.c","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate misconceptions about interest","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,51]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank sells a car on credit for 1,200 (cash price 1,000), payable in one year. When the customer is late, the bank adds 50 for the extra month. Which part is Riba?","options":["The 200 credit mark-up","The 50 added after the sale price was fixed","Both","Neither"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The price may reflect credit terms before execution; once the sale is executed the price is a debt that cannot increase.","topicId":"t3.2.1.b","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on credit prices","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.4","type":"match","prompt":"Match each exchange to the applicable rule.","pairs":[["Gold for gold","Equal and spot"],["US Dollars for Yen","Any rate, but spot"],["Dollars for a car","Excess and delay both allowed"]],"explanation":"Nawavi’s three rules (p. 52).","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply exchange rules","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.5","type":"multi","prompt":"Which are EXCLUDED from Riba under the broad definition given in Section 3.2.1? (Select all.)","options":["Profit in trading","Commission in Wakalah","Rentals in Ijarah","Addition for extending a loan","Inflation indexation of a loan"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"Trading/Shirkah profit, Ujrah/Wakalah fees and Ijarah rentals correspond to liability and risk.","topicId":"t3.2.1.e","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the definition of Riba","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52,53]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.6","type":"identify","prompt":"“Riba also called Riba Al-Qur’an, relating to loan transactions.” Which type?","options":["Riba Al-Fadl","Riba Al-Nasiah","Riba al-Sarf","Riba al-Buyu"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Riba Al-Nasiah (p. 53).","topicId":"t3.2.1.e","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish types of Riba","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52,53]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What distinguishes prohibited Gharar from tolerable uncertainty?","options":["Any uncertainty is prohibited","Only excessive Gharar affecting principal aspects, not needed by a valid contract like Salam, is prohibited","Gharar only applies to insurance","Only uncertainty about price matters"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 59.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the extent of Gharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.8","type":"scenario","prompt":"A developer says: ‘I sell you one of the houses in this project for 5 million’ without identifying which house. Based on Chapter 3, this contract:","options":["Is valid","Involves Gharar due to ignorance of the specific identity of the object","Involves Riba Al-Fadl","Is Maisir only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"This exact example is given as Gharar (p. 60).","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify Gharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.9","type":"tf","prompt":"Gharar is considered less significant than Riba because some degree of uncertainty is acceptable while the slightest Riba invalidates a transaction.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 58.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare Gharar and Riba","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.10","type":"application","prompt":"A savings scheme keeps investors’ principal intact but gives large prizes by monthly draw funded from interest on the pooled money. The author regards it as:","options":["Permissible because principal is safe","Repugnant due to Riba and Maisir","Permissible if prizes are small","A form of Takaful"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 64.","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the prohibition of Maisir","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.11","type":"definition","prompt":"“Bidding up the price without an intention to take delivery of the commodity” is:","options":["Khalabah","Najash","Ghaban-e-Fahish","Jahl"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 66.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define ethical prohibitions","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.12","type":"mcq","prompt":"How are late-payment fines imposed by Islamic banks on wilful defaulters treated?","options":["Credited to profit and loss","Used for charitable purposes, not credited to P&L","Paid to depositors","Refunded after repayment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 66.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain treatment of penalties","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.13","type":"order","prompt":"Order the Qur’anic verses on Riba by revelation as listed by the author.","items":["30:39","4:161","3:130","2:275–281"],"explanation":"p. 44.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the sequence of revelation","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.14","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does the author contrast capitalism and Islam on money capital as a factor of production?","options":["Both treat it like land","Capitalism treats it on a par with land and labour (fixed return); Islam on a par with enterprise (return only with risk)","Islam gives it rent","Capitalism treats it as enterprise"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 55.","topicId":"t3.2.1.f","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Compare treatments of capital","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,57]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.15","type":"short","prompt":"Briefly explain why the author rejects the argument that interest is justified to compensate for inflation.","keywords":["depreciation","benefit","indexation","Riba"],"correctAnswer":"Depreciation affects money equally whether in the lender’s pocket or with the borrower; lending to gain from indexation is a loan seeking benefit, which involves Riba. Hence indexation of financial obligations also leads to Riba (the Fiqh Academy also forbids indexation).","explanation":"Section 3.2.1 misconceptions and Summary.","topicId":"t3.2.1.c","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate inflation-based arguments","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,51]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which pre-Islamic practice does the author link to the foundation of Takaful?","options":["Bai‘ al Hasat","Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq","Mulamasah","Habal-al-Hablah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Joint funds to indemnify route losses (p. 68).","topicId":"t3.3.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Link mutual cooperation to Takaful","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.3","pages":[68,68]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.17","type":"mcq","prompt":"When did the final and repeated prohibition of Riba come, according to the author?","options":["Before the Hijrah","Around the battle of Uhud, 3 AH","In 10 AH, about two weeks before the Prophet’s passing","After the conquest of Taif"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"Express prohibition around 3 AH; final in 10 AH (p. 44).","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the chronology of the prohibition","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.18","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the Hadith of Anas, a lender should not accept even a meal or a ride offered by the borrower unless such favours were customary between them before the loan.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Even small benefits from a debtor are avoided (pp. 46, 49).","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the rule on benefits from debtors","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.19","type":"identify","prompt":"Which Hadith is the basis of the juristic rules on Riba in exchange of commodities?","options":["The Hadith of Jabir on cursing all parties","The Hadith of the six commodities: gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates and salt","The Hadith on the wet grain","The Hadith of the boat"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Like for like, equal for equal, hand to hand (pp. 46, 51–52).","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the source of Riba Al-Fadl rules","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.20","type":"scenario","prompt":"A customer buys goods on credit at an agreed price of 1,100. At maturity he cannot pay, and the seller offers to extend the term for another 50. How does the author classify the 50?","options":["Legitimate profit on a new sale","Riba — once the price is fixed it becomes a debt that cannot increase","A permissible late fee retained by the seller","Musawamah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Verse 2:275 context: bargaining is allowed before the price is stipulated, not after (pp. 48–49).","topicId":"t3.2.1.b","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule against increasing a debt","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.21","type":"comparison","prompt":"Which statement correctly contrasts Bai‘, Qard and Ijarah?","options":["All three transfer ownership permanently","Sale transfers ownership permanently; a loan transfers it temporarily with return of the like; a lease transfers only usufruct while ownership stays with the lessor","A lease transfers ownership, a sale does not","A loan may carry rent"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 48.","topicId":"t3.2.1.b","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Contrast sale, loan and lease","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.22","type":"multi","prompt":"Which arguments for the permissibility of interest does the author reject? (Select all.)","options":["Only Riba al-jahiliyyah is prohibited","Only excessive rates are prohibited","Interest compensates for inflation","Commercial loans were unknown in Arabia","Trade profit is permitted"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3],"explanation":"Trade profit being permitted is not an argument for interest; it is the Qur’anic contrast itself (pp. 49–51).","topicId":"t3.2.1.c","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate arguments on interest","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,51]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.23","type":"application","prompt":"Someone argues that interest on a loan to a large, rich company cannot be unjust. What is the author’s reply?","options":["He agrees","It strengthens the case against interest: the rich get cheap funds, pass the cost to consumers and grow richer","Only if the rate is below 5%","Only if the company is foreign"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 51.","topicId":"t3.2.1.c","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the author’s rebuttal on rich debtors","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,51]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.24","type":"match","prompt":"Match each exchange to the rule under Imam Nawavi’s summary.","pairs":[["Wheat for wheat","Equal quantity and spot"],["Wheat for rice","Excess allowed, but spot"],["Gold for wheat","Excess and delay both allowed"],["Dollars for dollars","Equal and spot"]],"explanation":"Edibility is the ‘Illah for wheat/rice (p. 52).","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply exchange rules to new pairs","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.25","type":"tf","prompt":"The equal-and-spot rule for exchanging like monetary goods also applies to a Qard where the borrower returns the same amount later.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"The rule is for exchange/sale contracts; Qard and Dayn are exempt (p. 52, n. 21).","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Distinguish exchange from loan rules","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.26","type":"definition","prompt":"“The quality premium in the exchange of low-quality for better-quality goods of the same genus” defines:","options":["Riba Al-Nasiah","Riba Al-Fadl","Gharar","Ghaban"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 53.","topicId":"t3.2.1.e","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Riba Al-Fadl","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52,53]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.27","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which rationale for the prohibition of interest does the author find most convincing?","options":["Ritual obedience only","Prevention of luxury","Distributive justice — preventing wealth circulating only among the rich (59:7)","Ecological protection"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 54–55.","topicId":"t3.2.1.f","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Identify the preferred rationale","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,57]},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["q-ch3-12"],"explanationAlt":"Among several candidate rationales, Ayub identifies distributive justice as the most convincing: the prohibition of Riba is intended to prevent wealth accumulation in a few hands, so it does not circulate 'among the rich' (Qur'an 59:7)."},{"id":"q3.28","type":"order","prompt":"Order these historical steps in the Western acceptance of interest as described by the author.","items":["Ancient philosophers and the Testaments forbid interest","England (1545) splits a legal maximum “interest” from usury","Calvin and Molinaeus argue for interest in theory","The Industrial Revolution gives the movement momentum"],"explanation":"pp. 53–54.","topicId":"t3.2.1.f","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence the history of interest in the West","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,57]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.29","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why Islam treats money capital on a par with enterprise rather than with land and labour.","keywords":["consumed","risk","profit","loss","enterprise"],"correctAnswer":"Money is consumed in use and cannot be leased, so it cannot earn rent. The profit of a productive venture is unknown and a loss is possible; justice requires the capital provider to share the risk with the entrepreneur if he wants profit. Capitalism gives money capital a fixed return like land and labour; Islam gives it a share of profit only with liability for loss.","explanation":"pp. 55, 57.","topicId":"t3.2.1.f","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the treatment of money capital","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,57]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.30","type":"identify","prompt":"According to the Zahiri school (Ibn Hazm), Gharar in sales occurs when:","options":["Anything is doubtful","The buyer does not know what he bought and the seller does not know what he sold","Payment is deferred","Goods are imported"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Zahiris apply Gharar to the unknown, excluding the doubtful; the majority include both (p. 59).","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish juristic views on Gharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.31","type":"multi","prompt":"Which situations does Islamic law exclude from the power to sell in order to avoid Gharar? (Select all.)","options":["Things that do not exist","Things that exist but are not in the seller’s possession or whose availability is not expected","Things exchanged with uncertain delivery and payment","Goods sold at a disclosed profit"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 60.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the three restricted situations","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.32","type":"scenario","prompt":"A seller says: “I sell you whatever is in my pocket for 100.” What is the defect?","options":["Riba Al-Fadl","Gharar/Jahl — the subject matter is unknown","Najash","None"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Selling unknown goods is an example of Gharar (p. 61).","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Identify Gharar in a sale","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.33","type":"application","prompt":"A trader agrees to sell goods “at whatever the market price will be” with no further specification. Is this valid?","options":["Yes, market price is always fair","No — selling without specifying the price is listed as Gharar","Yes, if delivery is spot","Only for gold"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 61.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the certainty-of-price rule","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.34","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do the Qur’an and Hadith literature differ in the term they use for gambling?","options":["Both use Qimar","The Qur’an uses Maisir; Hadith literature generally uses Qimar","The Qur’an uses Gharar","Hadith uses Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Maisir in 2:219 and 5:90–91; Qimar in Hadith (p. 62).","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Maisir and Qimar terminology","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.35","type":"scenario","prompt":"A shop gives a free bottle of oil with every bag of rice, with no draw or prize. Is this gambling?","options":["Yes, it is a lottery","No — the buyer knows what he buys and the price; there is no element of chance","Only if the oil is expensive","Yes, it is Riba"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 63.","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish incentives from gambling","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.36","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the author, futures and options contracts settled only through price differences are covered by the prohibition of gambling.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 62.","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Apply gambling to derivatives","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.37","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does Ghaban-e-Fahish mean?","options":["Bidding without intention to buy","Selling at a much higher price while giving the impression it is the market rate","Hoarding","Misleading advertising"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 68.","topicId":"t3.3.4","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Ghaban-e-Fahish","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.4","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.38","type":"multi","prompt":"Which did the OIC Fiqh Academy (1988) state about markets? (Select all.)","options":["Traders are free to buy and sell within Shari’ah","There is no fixed limit on the percentage of profit","The government should fix all prices","Government may intervene when prices rise due to artificial factors"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3],"explanation":"p. 69.","topicId":"t3.3.4","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the Fiqh Academy market principles","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.4","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.39","type":"identify","prompt":"“Deceiving a Mustarsal is Riba.” Who is a Mustarsal?","options":["A broker","An unknowing entrant into the market","A money-changer","A guarantor"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 67.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall disclosure norms","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q3.40","type":"application","prompt":"Controlling shareholders plan a merger that harms minority shareholders. Which classical concept does the author suggest could be extended by analogy to protect them?","options":["Hawalah","Shuf‘ah (pre-emption)","Kafalah","Arbun"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 70.","topicId":"t3.3.5","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.5","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.5","pages":[69,70]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch3-1","type":"order","prompt":"Order these Qur'anic passages on Riba by their chronology of revelation, from earliest to final/most comprehensive.","explanation":"The order of revelation given in Chapter 3 is: Al-Rum 30:39 (mild disapproval), Al-Nisa 4:161, Al-e-Imran 3:130 (first express prohibition, ~Battle of Uhud), and finally Al-Baqarah 2:275-281 (10 AH, the most comprehensive and final prohibition).","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Riba: Qur'anic Revelation Chronology and Hadith Evidence","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the Qur'anic and Hadith basis for the prohibition of Riba and trace the chronology of its revelation","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,45]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"items":["Al-Rum 30:39","Al-Nisa 4:161","Al-e-Imran 3:130","Al-Baqarah 2:275-281"]},{"id":"q-ch3-2","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Hadith from Jabir say about the parties involved in a Riba transaction?","explanation":"The Hadith from Jabir states the Prophet cursed the receiver and payer of interest, the one who records it, and the witnesses to the transaction, declaring 'they are all alike [in guilt]'.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Riba: Qur'anic Revelation Chronology and Hadith Evidence","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the Qur'anic and Hadith basis for the prohibition of Riba and trace the chronology of its revelation","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[46]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only the lender is cursed","The Prophet cursed the receiver, payer, recorder and witnesses of interest, saying 'they are all alike [in guilt]'","Only the government regulator is responsible","It applies only to Riba above a certain rate"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-3","type":"short","prompt":"What historical dispute prompted the revelation of Qur'an 2:278-279, per Justice Taqi Usmani's account?","explanation":"Justice Taqi Usmani's account traces the revelation of 2:278-279 to the Banu Thaqif's attempt to retain asymmetric interest rights after converting to Islam, which the Prophet rejected, prompting the verses commanding all believers to give up outstanding Riba.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Riba: Qur'anic Revelation Chronology and Hadith Evidence","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Qur'anic and Hadith basis for the prohibition of Riba and trace the chronology of its revelation","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[45,46]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The Banu Thaqif dispute — after the conquest of Makkah, the Prophet voided outstanding Riba amounts; Banu Thaqif tried to retain their right to collect interest owed to them (via a treaty clause) while their own creditors forgave interest owed by them. When their debtors refused to pay, the matter reached the Prophet, prompting the revelation of these verses commanding believers to forgo all outstanding Riba."},{"id":"q-ch3-4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A trader sells goods on 12-month credit at $110, versus a $100 cash price, as the originally agreed and fixed sale price. Six months later, the buyer is late paying, and the trader wants to add a further $5 'late fee' to the outstanding amount. Which part(s) of this scenario are permissible or prohibited, per Chapter 3's Riba framework?","explanation":"The chapter's timing test: a profit margin embedded in the original sale price (the $110, bargained before the debt was fixed) is valid. Any addition made after the debt/liability is fixed (the $5 late fee) is Riba, per Qur'an 2:278-279 and the traditions on debt liability not increasing due to payment default.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Riba in Loans and Debts: Distinguishing Sale, Loan and Lease","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why a Qard (loan) must be repaid at exactly the principal, and how Bai' (sale), loan and Ijarah (lease) differ in ownership/risk transfer","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Both the $110 credit price and the $5 late fee are Riba","The $110 credit price (embedded in the original sale before the debt was fixed) is permissible; the additional $5 late fee (added after the debt/liability was fixed) is Riba","Neither is Riba, since the total is a reasonable markup","Only the $5 late fee is permissible; the $110 credit price is Riba"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can money not be the subject of an Ijarah (lease) contract, per Chapter 3?","explanation":"Anything that cannot be used without consuming its corpus, or that changes shape in the process of use (yarn, money, edibles, fuel), cannot be leased — this is precisely why charging 'rent' on money (interest) is prohibited, while renting a house or vehicle is permissible.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Riba in Loans and Debts: Distinguishing Sale, Loan and Lease","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why a Qard (loan) must be repaid at exactly the principal, and how Bai' (sale), loan and Ijarah (lease) differ in ownership/risk transfer","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because money has no value","Because Ijarah applies only to assets whose corpus is not consumed or altered by use, and money is consumed/changes form when used","Because money can only be given as a gift","Because Ijarah is only for real estate"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-6","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following misconceptions about Riba does Chapter 3 explicitly rebut?","explanation":"The chapter rebuts: (1) the 'only high rates' argument, (2) the 'only consumption loans' argument (via SAB's Byzantine-era evidence), (3) the 'inflation compensation' argument (rejected by the OIC Fiqh Academy), and (5) the 'rich borrowers aren't exploited' argument. Profit-sharing (e.g. Mudarabah/Musharakah) is not prohibited — it is the very alternative the book promotes.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Common Misconceptions About Riba and Their Rebuttal","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Rebut the common misconceptions used to argue that modern commercial interest is not Riba","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,50,51]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only high/'usurious' interest rates are prohibited","Only consumption loans (not commercial/productive loans) are covered by the prohibition","Interest is a fair compensation for inflation/loss of purchasing power","All forms of profit-sharing are prohibited","Interest charged on loans to wealthy borrowers is not exploitative"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4]},{"id":"q-ch3-7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What analogy does the Encyclopaedia of Islam (Urdu) use to refute the claim that only 'doubled and redoubled' (high) rates of Riba are prohibited?","explanation":"The Encyclopaedia argues that just as prohibiting sale of Allah's Signs 'for a low price' does not imply a high price is permitted, the mention of 'doubled and redoubled' Riba in 3:130 does not imply that a smaller addition over the principal is permitted.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Common Misconceptions About Riba and Their Rebuttal","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Rebut the common misconceptions used to argue that modern commercial interest is not Riba","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[50]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It compares interest rates to weather patterns","It notes that Qur'an 5:44 prohibits selling Allah's Signs 'for a low price' — which obviously does not permit selling them at a high price; similarly, 3:130's mention of doubled/redoubled Riba does not imply smaller amounts are permitted","It compares Riba to Zakat rates","It argues all Qur'anic numbers are purely symbolic and carry no legal weight"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-8","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The OIC Fiqh Academy permits indexing a lent amount of money to the cost of living or inflation rate, as long as the index is transparent.","explanation":"False. The OIC Fiqh Academy categorically ruled indexation of a lent amount to inflation, interest rates, GNP growth, gold prices, etc. as strictly forbidden (a form of Riba), since it seeks a benefit for the lender.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Common Misconceptions About Riba and Their Rebuttal","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Rebut the common misconceptions used to argue that modern commercial interest is not Riba","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch3-9","type":"scenario","prompt":"A person wants to exchange 100 US Dollars for Japanese Yen at the prevailing rate, with settlement to occur three days later (not on the spot). Per Imam Nawavi's exchange rules, is this permissible?","explanation":"Per Imam Nawavi's rules, when commodities of exchange are heterogeneous but share the same 'Illah (as currencies do, both serving as units of value/Thamaniyyah), excess/deficiency in amount is allowed, but delay in exchange is not — settlement must be hand to hand (on the spot).","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Riba al-Fadl: Riba in Sale/Exchange Transactions, and the Full Connotation of Riba","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State the Qur'anic and Hadith basis for the prohibition of Riba and trace the chronology of its revelation","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, fully permissible with no restrictions","No — USD and JPY share the same 'Illah (both are monetary/Thamaniyyah units) but are different genus, so excess/deficiency is allowed but delay is prohibited; the exchange must be hand to hand","No — this is never permissible under any circumstances","Yes, because different currencies have completely different 'Illah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-10","type":"identify","prompt":"Identify which six commodities are named in the foundational Hadith establishing Riba al-Fadl rules.","explanation":"The Hadith names: 'Gold for gold, silver for silver, wheat for wheat, barley for barley, dates for dates and salt for salt — like for like, equal for equal, and hand to hand.'","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Riba al-Fadl: Riba in Sale/Exchange Transactions, and the Full Connotation of Riba","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Riba al-Nasiah (Riba in loans/debts) from Riba al-Fadl (Riba in sale/exchange transactions), with examples","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[46,51]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, salt","Gold, silver, rice, corn, apples, sugar","Oil, gas, wheat, barley, dates, salt","Gold, silver, wheat, cattle, dates, salt"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch3-11","type":"short","prompt":"Give the chapter's comprehensive, functional definition of Riba.","explanation":"This definition requires all accruals to correspond to liability and risk, and excludes legitimate trading/Shirkah profit, Ujrah/Wakalah service fees, and Ijarah rentals from being classified as Riba.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Riba al-Fadl: Riba in Sale/Exchange Transactions, and the Full Connotation of Riba","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Riba al-Nasiah (Riba in loans/debts) from Riba al-Fadl (Riba in sale/exchange transactions), with examples","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"Riba means and includes any increase over and above the principal amount payable in a contract obligation, not covered by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise."},{"id":"q-ch3-13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A bank argues that charging interest on a business loan is fine because the borrower is a profitable company, not a poor consumer. How does Chapter 3 respond to this type of argument?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly rejects the consumption-vs-productive-loan distinction: since a productive investment's profit is not known beforehand and loss is possible, a fixed predetermined interest rate cannot be morally justified — justice demands risk-sharing for any profit entitlement.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"The Rationale for Prohibiting Interest: Distributive Justice and Economic Evidence","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the author's stated rationale (distributive justice) for the prohibition of interest, and how it differs from conventional treatment of capital","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[55]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It agrees — interest is only prohibited for consumption loans by the poor","It rejects this: interest on productive loans is prohibited too, since the venture's profit outcome is inherently uncertain, so a fixed interest rate cannot be morally justified — the capital provider must share risk to earn any return","It is irrelevant to Islamic finance","It agrees, since profitable companies can always repay"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-14","type":"short","prompt":"What did Kester's (1986) study find about debt-to-equity ratios in the US and Japan, and what claim does it rebut?","explanation":"Kester's findings are cited to counter the conventional claim that interest-based credit is essential for productive investment.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"The Rationale for Prohibiting Interest: Distributive Justice and Economic Evidence","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the author's stated rationale (distributive justice) for the prohibition of interest, and how it differs from conventional treatment of capital","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[56]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Debt-to-equity ratios for major business categories in the US and Japan were substantially below unity, meaning equity financing was much more prevalent than debt financing — this rebuts the popular misconception that credit/debt plays a critical role in modern investment and business spending."},{"id":"q-ch3-15","type":"comparison","prompt":"Contrast how a joint business venture's losses are distributed among capital, land and labour providers if it fails, per Chapter 3.","explanation":"Per Chapter 3: if a joint business fails, the capital provider loses their money, the land provider loses rent, and the labour provider's labour is 'wasted' (loses wages) — this is the practical mechanism behind the principle that capital's return is uniquely risk-contingent.","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","topicLabel":"Riba and Factor Payments: Why Money-Capital Cannot Earn a Guaranteed Return","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the author's stated rationale (distributive justice) for the prohibition of interest, and how it differs from conventional treatment of capital","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[57]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["All three providers share losses equally regardless of role","The capital provider loses their money, the land provider loses rent, and the labour provider loses wages — each factor bears loss specific to its own role, but only capital's return is contingent on the business's overall success","Only the labour provider bears any loss","The government compensates all providers for any loss"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-16","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is not all uncertainty prohibited in Islamic business transactions?","explanation":"The chapter explains that scholars distinguish Gharar-e-Kathir (excessive, prohibited) from Gharar Qalil (nominal, tolerated), since total certainty is unattainable in business and risk-bearing is itself required for legitimate profit.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Gharar: Excessive Uncertainty in Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Define Gharar, distinguish excessive (Kathir) from minor (Qalil) uncertainty, and list categories/examples of Gharar-vitiated sales","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,58]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because uncertainty is always beneficial to sellers","Because total certainty is impossible in any business, and risk-bearing is itself a condition for legitimate profit entitlement — only excessive (Kathir) uncertainty affecting the contract's principal aspects is prohibited","Because Gharar rules only apply to gold and silver","Because uncertainty was never discussed in early Islamic jurisprudence"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-17","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are examples of Gharar given in Chapter 3?","explanation":"Fish in water, contents of a sealed box, and Bai' al Hasat (pebble sale) are all classic Gharar examples given in the chapter. A house sold at a clearly stated fixed price for immediate payment, or wheat sold with clear price/immediate delivery, are ordinary valid sales with no Gharar.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Gharar: Excessive Uncertainty in Contracts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Gharar, distinguish excessive (Kathir) from minor (Qalil) uncertainty, and list categories/examples of Gharar-vitiated sales","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[58,60]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Selling fish still in the water","Selling a house at a clearly stated fixed price for immediate cash payment","Selling the contents of a sealed box without knowing what's inside","Selling cloth determined by where a thrown stone/pebble lands (Bai' al Hasat)","Selling wheat for an agreed cash price with immediate delivery"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch3-18","type":"scenario","prompt":"A seller offers: 'I will sell you my laptop for $500 cash today, or $550 if you pay in three months — you decide later which price applies, and the sale is binding on you either way.' What Gharar issue does this raise, per Chapter 3?","explanation":"This matches the chapter's example of prohibited 'two sales in one': two different prices are given for one article (cash vs credit) without specifying at which price the buyer purchases, while the sale remains binding at either price — this is Gharar because the actual sale price is not specified/known.","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Gharar: Excessive Uncertainty in Contracts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Define Gharar, distinguish excessive (Kathir) from minor (Qalil) uncertainty, and list categories/examples of Gharar-vitiated sales","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[60,61]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No issue — this is a normal credit sale","This is a 'two sales in one' type of Gharar: the sale price is not specified at the time of contracting, since neither party knows which of the two prices will apply","This is Maisir, not Gharar","This is a valid Salam contract"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-19","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why is gambling classified as a form of Gharar?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states: 'Gambling is a form of Gharar because the gambler is ignorant of the result of the gamble.'","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Maisir/Qimar: Gambling, Lotteries and Prize Schemes","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Maisir/Qimar and identify which financial schemes (lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, price-difference-settled futures/options) fall under this prohibition","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,62]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because gambling always involves money","Because the gambler is ignorant of the result of the gamble","Because gambling is a modern invention","Because gambling only involves games of physical skill"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-20","type":"scenario","prompt":"A charitable foundation with equally-qualified candidates for a single scholarship decides the winner by drawing lots, since all other criteria are tied and no candidate has any pre-existing claim to the scholarship. Is this a permissible use of chance, per Chapter 3?","explanation":"Per Chapter 3's test, valid lot-drawing occurs when no participant has a personal/vested right in the matter and no one is deprived of something already possessed/contributed — deciding among equally-qualified candidates for a single benefit by lot fits this test.","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Maisir/Qimar: Gambling, Lotteries and Prize Schemes","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Define Maisir/Qimar and identify which financial schemes (lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, price-difference-settled futures/options) fall under this prohibition","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[62,63]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["No, all drawing of lots is prohibited","Yes — no participant has a pre-existing vested right and no one is deprived of something they already possessed, making this a neutral, fair allocation method among equals","No, because money is involved","Yes, but only if a fee is charged"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-21","type":"multi","prompt":"Which modern financial practices does Chapter 3 identify as falling under the Maisir/Qimar prohibition?","explanation":"The chapter identifies lotteries/draws, conventional insurance, prize bond schemes, and price-difference-settled futures/options as falling under Maisir/Qimar (often combined with Riba). Musharakah is a legitimate risk-sharing partnership structure, not a prohibited practice.","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","topicLabel":"Prohibition of Maisir/Qimar: Gambling, Lotteries and Prize Schemes","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define Maisir/Qimar and identify which financial schemes (lotteries, prize bonds, conventional insurance, price-difference-settled futures/options) fall under this prohibition","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[63,64]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Government/corporate lotteries and draws for fundraising","Conventional insurance (due to combined Riba and Maisir)","Musharakah profit-and-loss-sharing partnerships","Bank prize bond schemes funded from pooled-capital interest","Futures/options contracts settled purely by price differences without genuine delivery intent"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4]},{"id":"q-ch3-22","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Qur'an 5:8 as cited in Chapter 3, toward whom must justice be maintained in business/economic dealings?","explanation":"Qur'an 5:8 explicitly commands justice even toward those one has enmity/hatred for, establishing justice as an unconditional obligation.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","topicLabel":"Business Ethics I: Justice, Honesty, and the Prohibitions of Najash, Khalabah and Non-Disclosure","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Only toward fellow Muslims","Only toward business partners","Even toward one's enemies — 'let not the enmity and hatred of others make you avoid justice; be just, that is nearest to piety'","Only toward the wealthy"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch3-23","type":"short","prompt":"Define Najash and state the Hadith describing its penalty.","explanation":"Najash is explicitly prohibited and equated in severity with taking Riba.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","topicLabel":"Business Ethics I: Justice, Honesty, and the Prohibitions of Najash and Khalabah and Non-Disclosure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[65,66]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"Najash is bidding up a price without any intention to purchase or take delivery of the commodity, to manipulate other genuine buyers into paying more. The Prophet said: 'A Najish (one who serves as an agent to bid up the price in an auction) is a cursed taker of Riba.'"},{"id":"q-ch3-24","type":"scenario","prompt":"An online seller writes a product description that exaggerates a used phone's condition as 'like new' when it actually has significant scratches and a cracked screen, to attract naive buyers unfamiliar with market conditions. Which Chapter 3 prohibition does this most directly violate?","explanation":"This scenario directly violates the prohibition of Khalabah (misleading marketing/overprojecting quality) and the duty to disclose known defects; deceiving an unknowing entrant (Mustarsal) into the market is described in Hadith as equivalent to Riba.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","topicLabel":"Business Ethics I: Justice, Honesty, and the Prohibitions of Najash and Khalabah and Non-Disclosure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[66,67]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Maisir/Qimar","Khalabah (misleading marketing) and non-disclosure of known defects — deceiving a Mustarsal (unknowing market entrant) is equated with Riba","Riba al-Fadl","Shuf'ah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-25","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Islamic banks are Shari'ah-permitted to impose penalty fines on wilful loan defaulters, provided the fine proceeds go to charity rather than the bank's own profit.","explanation":"True — Shari'ah boards have permitted Islamic banks to impose fines on wilful defaulters as a deterrent, with the fine amount directed to charitable purposes, not credited to the bank's own P&L account, distinguishing this from charging Riba.","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","topicLabel":"Business Ethics I: Justice, Honesty, and the Prohibitions of Najash and Khalabah and Non-Disclosure","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[66]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":true},{"id":"q-ch3-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two pre-Islamic customs does Chapter 3 identify as the historical foundation of the modern Takaful institution?","explanation":"'Aqilah (tribal shared responsibility for calamity/blood-money) and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (pooled indemnification of trade-route losses) are the pre-Islamic mutual-help customs validated by Islam and identified as Takaful's historical foundation.","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","topicLabel":"Business Ethics II: Covenants, Mutual Cooperation, Fair Pricing and Freedom from Dharar","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[67,68]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Bai' al Sarf and Bai' al Gharar","'Aqilah and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq","Khiyar al-Majlis and Khiyar al 'Aib","Musharakah and Mudarabah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-27","type":"scenario","prompt":"A well-intentioned merchant decides to sell rice at a price well below the prevailing market rate purely out of generosity. Based on the Caliph Umar precedent in Chapter 3, how might this be treated?","explanation":"The chapter cites Caliph Umar requiring a below-market seller to raise the price to the market level or leave the market — even philanthropic underpricing can create problems for other legitimate sellers and future supply.","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","topicLabel":"Business Ethics II: Covenants, Mutual Cooperation, Fair Pricing and Freedom from Dharar","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Encouraged without limit as an act of charity","The Caliph could require the merchant to raise the price to market level or leave the market, since below-market selling can distort future supply and harm other genuine sellers","Prohibited entirely as Riba","Irrelevant to Islamic business ethics"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-28","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: The OIC Fiqh Academy's 1988 ruling sets a fixed maximum profit percentage that Islamic traders may charge.","explanation":"False. The ruling explicitly states there is no restriction on the percentage of profit a trader may make — it is left to the merchants, the business environment and the nature of the goods, subject to Shari'ah-recommended ethics.","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","topicLabel":"Business Ethics II: Covenants, Mutual Cooperation, Fair Pricing and Freedom from Dharar","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch3-29","type":"comparison","prompt":"How might Shuf'ah (the pre-emptive right of a partner in joint ownership) be analogously extended to modern joint-stock companies, per the author's suggestion?","explanation":"The author suggests Shuf'ah's underlying principle (protecting a party from harm caused by others' decisions regarding jointly-held property) could extend by analogy to protecting minority shareholders from harmful controlling-shareholder decisions.","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","topicLabel":"Business Ethics II: Covenants, Mutual Cooperation, Fair Pricing and Freedom from Dharar","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[69,70]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It has no possible modern application","By analogy, it could protect minority shareholders from being adversely affected by controlling shareholders' unilateral decisions, such as dilutive share issuances, management changes, or mergers/acquisitions","By requiring all shareholders to have exactly equal voting rights","By abolishing joint-stock companies entirely"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch3-30","type":"short","prompt":"Why are unilateral promises treated as binding in Islamic finance, and name two financing modes where this matters.","explanation":"The binding nature of unilateral promises underpins several core Islamic financing structures discussed later in the book.","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","topicLabel":"Business Ethics II: Covenants, Mutual Cooperation, Fair Pricing and Freedom from Dharar","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"List and explain the core Islamic business ethics norms: justice, honesty, prohibition of Najash and Khalabah, disclosure, covenant-fulfilment, mutual cooperation, fair pricing, and freedom from Dharar","source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[67]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because Shari'ah emphasizes fulfilment not only of formal bilateral contracts but also of promises/unilateral undertakings (Qur'an 17:34), and contemporary scholars unanimously consider such promises binding. This matters in Murabaha to Purchase Orderer, leasing (Ijarah), and Diminishing Musharakah, where a promisee can recover actual loss if the promisor reneges."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f3.1","front":"Qur’anic principle for loans and debts (2:278–279)","back":"The creditor is entitled to the principal (Ra’as al-mal) only; any amount, big or small, over and above it is Riba.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.2","front":"Riba Al-Nasiah","back":"Excess/benefit arising from delay of a counter value in an exchange based on loans or sales; also called Riba Al-Qur’an.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1.e","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52,53]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.3","front":"Riba Al-Fadl","back":"Quality premium/excess in exchanging goods of the same genus (e.g. dates for dates) — based on the six-commodities Hadith.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.4","front":"Broad definition of Riba (Ch. 3)","back":"Any increase over the principal payable in a contract obligation not covered by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1.e","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52,53]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.5","front":"Exchange rule: same commodity (gold for gold)","back":"Excess prohibited AND delay prohibited — equal for equal, hand to hand.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.6","front":"Exchange rule: different commodities, same ‘Illah (USD for JPY)","back":"Excess allowed; delay NOT allowed (spot exchange).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.7","front":"Exchange rule: different ‘Illah (dollars for a car)","back":"Both excess and delay permissible.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.8","front":"Sale vs loan vs lease — ownership","back":"Sale: permanent transfer at execution. Loan: temporary; return same/similar; no charge. Lease: ownership stays with lessor; usufruct for rent.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t3.2.1.b","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.9","front":"Gharar","back":"Uncertainty/hazard from lack of clarity about the subject matter or price (e.g. fish in water, bird in air). Only excessive Gharar (Gharar-e-Kathir) is prohibited.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.10","front":"When does Gharar make a contract prohibited?","back":"When it is major, affects the principal aspects of the contract and is not a need of a valid contract such as Salam or Istisna‘a.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.11","front":"Jahl","back":"Ignorance or non-clarity about the parties, rights and obligations, goods or price — part of Gharar.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.12","front":"Maisir vs Qimar","back":"Maisir: acquiring something valuable with ease by chance without compensation, work or liability. Qimar: gaining at others’ cost by chance — an important kind of Maisir.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.13","front":"Why are conventional prize bonds prohibited?","back":"Prizes are precalculated interest on pooled capital distributed by chance at other bondholders’ cost — involving both Riba and Maisir.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.14","front":"Najash","back":"Bidding up a price without intention to buy — prohibited; “a Najish is a cursed taker of Riba.”","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.15","front":"Khalabah","back":"Misleading marketing — overprojecting quality to unaware clients; includes misleading advertising.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.16","front":"Remedy for breach of a binding promise in Islamic finance","back":"The promisee may recover the actual loss incurred (used in MPO, leasing, Diminishing Musharakah).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[67,68]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.17","front":"‘Aqilah and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq","back":"Pre-Islamic mutual-help schemes (shared tribal liability; joint fund for route losses) validated by Islam — foundation of Takaful.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t3.3.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.3","pages":[68,68]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.18","front":"OIC Fiqh Academy (1988) on profit margin","back":"No restriction on the percentage of profit; ethics of moderation, contentment and leniency apply; price fixing only to remove artificial distortions.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.3.4","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.4","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.19","front":"Rationale for Riba prohibition preferred by the author","back":"Distributive justice — preventing accumulation of wealth in few hands (59:7).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1.f","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,57]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.20","front":"Fines on wilful defaulters in Islamic banks","back":"Allowed by Shari’ah boards as a deterrent; the amount is used for charity and not credited to the bank’s P&L.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.21","front":"Order of revelation of the verses on Riba","back":"30:39 → 4:161 → 3:130 (first express prohibition, around Uhud) → 2:275–281 (final, 10 AH).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.22","front":"Qard vs Dayn","back":"Qard: money/fungible lent, returned in like on demand. Dayn: liability from a credit transaction payable at an agreed time. Creditor gets the principal only in both.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.23","front":"Earnings: permitted vs prohibited (Qur’anic contrast)","back":"Profit from trade/business with liability — permitted. Return on cash (or its converted form) without liability — prohibited (Riba).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.24","front":"What cannot be leased, and why?","back":"Things consumed or transformed in use — money, yarn, edibles, fuel. They may be sold, but only assets whose corpus remains can be leased.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.1.b","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.25","front":"Justinian and commercial interest","back":"The Byzantine emperor Justinian (527–565) regulated interest rates on commercial loans — evidence that commercial lending was known to Makkan traders.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1.c","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49,51]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.26","front":"The modern ‘Illah for Riba rules","back":"Being used as money: Riba rules apply to anything serving the function of money — gold, silver, paper currency, IOUs.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.27","front":"England, 1545","back":"Riba was split into a legal maximum called “interest” and the excess (usury); later Calvin and Molinaeus justified interest in theory.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1.f","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[53,57]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.28","front":"Gharar-e-Kathir vs Gharar Qalil","back":"Excessive vs nominal uncertainty. Only excessive uncertainty about the subject matter or price prohibits a contract.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.29","front":"Habal-al-Hablah, Mulamasah, Munabadhah","back":"Sale of the unborn animal; sale by touching; barter without inspection — Gharar-based sales named in Bukhari’s chapter.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.30","front":"Bai‘ al-Ghaib","back":"Sale of absent or concealed goods, brought within Gharar by Ibn Abbas; the buyer has an option to revoke on sight.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.31","front":"When is drawing lots permissible?","back":"When people of equal standing must be chosen for a right or burden and no one loses what they contributed — not when some gain at others’ cost.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.32","front":"Ghaban and Ghaban-e-Fahish","back":"Misrepresenting goods or prices; charging a much higher price while implying it is the market rate.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.3.4","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.4","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f3.33","front":"Dharar and Shuf‘ah","back":"Freedom from harm to third parties; pre-emption right of a co-owner, which may be extended to protect minority shareholders.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.3.5","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.5","pages":[69,70]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-1","front":"Is there disagreement among Muslims about whether Riba is prohibited?","back":"No — there is no disagreement that Riba is prohibited (grounded in explicit Qur'an/Sunnah condemnation). The only disagreement is over what exactly constitutes Riba.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-2","front":"Order the four key Qur'anic Riba verses by revelation chronology and name the earliest and final ones.","back":"Al-Rum 30:39 (earliest, mild disapproval) → Al-Nisa 4:161 → Al-e-Imran 3:130 (first express prohibition, ~Battle of Uhud) → Al-Baqarah 2:275-281 (final, comprehensive prohibition, 10 AH, weeks before the Prophet's death).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,45]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-3","front":"What does Qur'an 2:275 say about trade versus Riba?","back":"'Allah has permitted trade and forbidden Riba' — directly refuting the usurers' claim that 'trade is just like interest'.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[45]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-4","front":"What did the Prophet declare at his Farewell Hajj regarding Riba, and whose debt did he cancel first?","back":"He declared 'every form of Riba is cancelled; capital indeed is yours which you shall have' and began by cancelling the Riba owed to his own uncle, Abbas — establishing the prohibition's universality and impartiality.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[46]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-5","front":"Define Qard and Dayn, and state the core rule limiting a creditor's recovery in each.","back":"Qard = a loan (money/goods to be returned identically, always free of charge). Dayn = a debt arising from a credit transaction (e.g. credit sale, accrued Ijarah rent). In both, per Qur'an 2:278-279, the creditor may recover only the Ra'asul-mal (principal) — any addition is Riba.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[47,48]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-6","front":"What is the key 'timing test' that distinguishes a legitimate credit-sale profit margin from Riba?","back":"A profit margin embedded in the original sale price (before the debt/liability is fixed) is valid. Any addition made after the price/debt has been fixed (e.g. for late payment) is Riba.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-7","front":"Why can money not be leased (Ijarah) for 'rent', while a house can?","back":"Ijarah applies only to assets whose corpus/original form is not consumed or altered by use. Money is consumed or changes form when used, so it cannot be leased — only exchanged via a valid sale. A house retains its form during use and can therefore be rented.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[49]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-8","front":"What historical evidence did Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench use to refute the claim that only 'consumption loans' were targeted by the Riba prohibition?","back":"It documented that commercial/industrial/agricultural interest-based loans were prevalent in the Byzantine Empire ruling Syria (Emperor Justinian even legislated permissible interest rates by borrower type), and that Makkan Arab traders had extensive commercial relations with Byzantine Syria — refuting the claim that commercial interest lending was unknown to the Prophet's contemporaries.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[50,51]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-9","front":"Is indexing a loan/debt to inflation, GNP growth or gold prices permitted in Islamic finance?","back":"No — the OIC Fiqh Academy categorically ruled indexation of a lent amount to inflation/cost of living/interest rates/GNP/gold prices as Riba, since it seeks a benefit for the lender. One may lend in a less inflation-vulnerable currency/gold, but the nominal debt liability itself cannot be increased.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-10","front":"Distinguish Riba al-Nasiah from Riba al-Fadl.","back":"Riba al-Nasiah: Riba of delay, arising in loan/debt transactions (present in all modern bank interest). Riba al-Fadl: the quality/quantity premium in exchanging low-quality for better-quality goods of the same genus (e.g. dates for dates), governed by exchange rules in sale/barter transactions.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52,53]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-11","front":"State Imam Nawavi's three-tier rule for exchanging commodities (Bai' al Sarf), based on 'Illah.","back":"1) Different 'Illah (e.g. gold for wheat): excess and delay both permitted. 2) Same genus/same 'Illah (e.g. gold for gold): excess and delay both prohibited — must be equal and immediate. 3) Same 'Illah, different genus (e.g. gold for silver, USD for JPY): excess/deficiency allowed, but delay prohibited.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-12","front":"Give the chapter's comprehensive, functional definition of Riba.","back":"Any increase over and above the principal amount payable in a contract obligation, not covered by a corresponding increase in labour, commodity, risk or expertise — this excludes legitimate trading/Shirkah profit, Ujrah/Wakalah fees, and Ijarah rentals from being Riba.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[52]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-13","front":"Where did the modern 'usury vs interest' legal distinction historically originate?","back":"In a 1545 English legal division by the King and Church, splitting interest charges into a 'legal maximum' (termed interest, permitted) and anything above it (retained as prohibited usury) — a historical/legal convenience, not a principled religious distinction.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[54]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-14","front":"What rationale does Ayub identify as the most convincing basis for prohibiting interest?","back":"Distributive justice — the prohibition of Riba is intended to prevent wealth accumulating in a few hands, i.e. to stop wealth from 'circulating among the rich' (Qur'an 59:7).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[54,55]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-15","front":"Why is interest on productive/commercial loans prohibited, not just interest on consumption loans?","back":"Because a productive investment's profit outcome is inherently uncertain (with possible loss) — a fixed, predetermined interest rate cannot be morally justified. Justice requires the capital provider to share risk with the entrepreneur to earn any return.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[55]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-16","front":"What did Kester (1986) find about debt-to-equity ratios in the US and Japan, and why does Ayub cite it?","back":"Debt-to-equity ratios for major business categories in the US and Japan were substantially below unity (equity financing more prevalent than debt) — cited to rebut the conventional claim that credit/debt plays a critical role in modern investment financing.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[56]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-17","front":"Contrast how capitalism and Islamic economics treat money-capital as a factor of production.","back":"Capitalism: money capital is treated like labour/land — entitled to a return (interest) regardless of profit or loss. Islamic economics: money capital is treated on a par with enterprise — entitled only to risk-contingent profit; the capital provider bears loss if the business fails.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[55,57]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-18","front":"Define Gharar and give its two classic legal examples.","back":"Gharar is uncertainty/hazard from lack of clarity about a contract's subject matter or price. Classic examples: selling fish still in water, or a bird still in the air.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,58]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-19","front":"Distinguish Gharar-e-Kathir from Gharar Qalil, and explain why this distinction matters.","back":"Gharar-e-Kathir = excessive/major uncertainty affecting the contract's principal aspects (prohibited). Gharar Qalil = nominal/minor uncertainty (tolerated). This matters because total certainty is impossible in business, and risk-bearing is itself required for legitimate profit entitlement.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[58]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-20","front":"What three categories of things does Islamic law deny the power to sell, to avoid Gharar?","back":"1) Things that do not exist as objects of a legal transaction; 2) things that exist but are not in the seller's possession or reasonably expected to be available; 3) things exchanged on an uncertain-delivery/payment basis.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[60]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-21","front":"Distinguish Maisir from Qimar.","back":"Maisir = acquisition of wealth by chance/easily, without equivalent compensation, work, or undertaking liability. Qimar = a specific, important subset of Maisir: receiving money/benefit at another's direct cost via a chance-based arrangement.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,62]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-22","front":"What test distinguishes a permissible lot-drawing from prohibited gambling?","back":"Permissible: no participant has a pre-existing personal/vested right at stake, and no one is deprived of something they already possessed or contributed (neutral, fair allocation among equals). Prohibited: the scheme causes loss to some participants for others' disproportionate gain.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[62,63]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-23","front":"Why are bank prize bond schemes ruled repugnant to Shari'ah?","back":"Because investors' principal may be safe, but the prizes are funded from interest generated on the pooled capital and distributed by chance to a few bondholders at the expense of the rest — combining both Riba and Maisir.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[63,64]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-24","front":"Define Najash and its Hadith-based penalty.","back":"Najash is bidding up a price with no intention of purchasing, to manipulate other genuine buyers into paying more. The Prophet said: 'A Najish is a cursed taker of Riba.'","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[65,66]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-25","front":"Define Khalabah and give an example of the modern practice it covers.","back":"Khalabah is misleading marketing — overprojecting a commodity's quality to unaware/simple clients. It covers modern misleading advertising; excessive oath-swearing while selling is also discouraged for the same reason.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[66]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-26","front":"What does the Hadith 'Deceiving a Mustarsal is Riba' establish?","back":"That deceiving a Mustarsal (an unknowing/naive market entrant) is equated in severity to Riba — an unusually strong equation used to stress the gravity of exploiting an information-disadvantaged buyer.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[67]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-27","front":"What pre-Islamic customs does Chapter 3 identify as the historical root of modern Takaful?","back":"'Aqilah (tribal shared responsibility for calamity/blood-money payment) and Dhaman Khatr al-Tariq (pooled funds indemnifying traders' journey losses from route hazards) — both validated by Islam as legitimate mutual-help mechanisms.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[67,68]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-28","front":"Per the OIC Fiqh Academy's 1988 ruling, is there a fixed cap on a trader's profit margin in Islamic finance?","back":"No — there is no fixed restriction on the percentage of profit a trader may make; it is left to merchants, the business environment and the nature of the goods, subject to Shari'ah-recommended ethics (moderation, contentment, leniency).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[68,69]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-29","front":"When is government price-fixing permitted, per the OIC Fiqh Academy's ruling in Chapter 3?","back":"Only when obvious market pitfalls or artificially-caused price distortions are identified; the government should then intervene specifically to remove the causal factors (fraud, hoarding, impaired competition) — not to override genuine, undistorted market pricing.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[69]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch3-30","front":"Define Shuf'ah and its relevance to freedom from Dharar.","back":"Shuf'ah is the pre-emptive right of a partner in joint ownership (or a neighbour, in sale of landed property). It illustrates freedom from Dharar (protecting a third party from harm caused by a contract between two other consenting parties); the author suggests it could analogously protect minority shareholders from harmful decisions by controlling shareholders.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t3.3.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.2","pages":[69,70]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e3.1","kind":"long","prompt":"What is Riba? Discuss its types, textual basis and why modern commercial interest falls within its scope.","expectedStructure":["Literal and technical meaning","Qur’anic verses (3:130, 2:275–281) and key Hadiths","Principal-only rule for Qard and Dayn","Riba Al-Nasiah vs Riba Al-Fadl","Broad definition and exclusions","Why commercial interest is Riba; rebut rate/purpose arguments","Rationale: distributive justice"],"keyConcepts":["Ra’as al-mal","Riba Al-Nasiah","Riba Al-Fadl","Bai‘ al Sarf","‘Illah"],"essentialPoints":["Any excess over principal","Trade permitted, Riba forbidden","Six commodities rule","Currencies: spot exchange","Any rate doubles over time"],"commonMistakes":["Equating Riba only with high rates","Omitting Riba in exchange","Calling credit-sale mark-up Riba"],"topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between sale, loan and lease in terms of ownership, risk and permissibility of return.","expectedStructure":["Sale","Loan","Lease","Table","Why money cannot be leased"],"keyConcepts":["ownership transfer","usufruct","principal"],"essentialPoints":["Sale — permanent, profit allowed","Loan — temporary, no charge","Lease — ownership retained, rent allowed, lessor bears ownership risk"],"commonMistakes":["Saying lessee bears ownership risk"],"topicId":"t3.2.1.b","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[48,49]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.3","kind":"short","prompt":"Define Gharar. What are its main forms and when does it render a contract invalid?","expectedStructure":["Definition with examples","Excessive vs minor","Al-Dhareer’s classification","Tests for prohibition","Contrast with Riba"],"keyConcepts":["Gharar-e-Kathir","Jahl","deliverability"],"essentialPoints":["Fish in water","Major, principal aspects, not needed by Salam/Istisna‘a"],"commonMistakes":["Treating all risk as Gharar"],"topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.4","kind":"scenario","prompt":"A bank launches a deposit scheme with a monthly lucky draw offering a car, funded from the scheme’s interest earnings. Analyse it using Chapter 3.","expectedStructure":["Identify Riba element","Identify Maisir/Qimar element","Apply the lottery test","Conclusion and permissible alternative (profit-based return)"],"keyConcepts":["Maisir","Qimar","Riba"],"essentialPoints":["Prize = interest distributed by chance","Loss to majority for gain of few","Diverts resources from real sector"],"commonMistakes":["Concluding it is valid because principal is safe"],"topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Can US Dollars be exchanged for Pakistani Rupees with settlement after one week?","expectedStructure":["Identify ‘Illah","Apply rule"],"keyConcepts":["Bai‘ al Sarf","‘Illah"],"essentialPoints":["Different currencies, same ‘Illah → any rate, but spot; delay not allowed"],"commonMistakes":["Requiring equal amounts"],"topicId":"t3.2.1.d","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[51,52]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.6","kind":"short","prompt":"Explain Najash, Khalabah and the importance of disclosure in Islamic business ethics.","expectedStructure":["Define each","Hadith references","Link to Gharar and Jahl","Implication for markets/investors"],"keyConcepts":["Najash","Khalabah","Mustarsal","transparency"],"essentialPoints":["Right to rescind for disadvantaged party","Information equally accessible"],"commonMistakes":["Confusing Najash and Khalabah"],"topicId":"t3.3.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.3.1","pages":[64,67]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.7","kind":"long","prompt":"Define Gharar and classify its forms. Why is some Gharar tolerated while no Riba is?","expectedStructure":["Meaning: hazard, uncertainty (Khatar)","Juristic definitions: Malik, Ibn Hazm, majority","Al-Dhareer’s classification: in the contract terms / in the object","Three situations where sale is denied","Excessive vs nominal Gharar; Salam and Istisna as need-based exceptions","Contrast with Riba’s zero tolerance"],"keyConcepts":["Gharar-e-Kathir","Jahl","Bai‘ al-Ghaib","two sales in one","Salam"],"essentialPoints":["Uncertainty cannot be avoided entirely","Prohibited Gharar is major, remunerative and affects principal aspects","Salam conditions remove Gharar","Derivatives involve major Gharar"],"commonMistakes":["Equating Gharar with ordinary business risk","Forgetting Jahl","Saying all uncertainty is prohibited"],"topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57,61]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e3.8","kind":"viva","prompt":"A bank’s prize bond keeps the principal safe but pays prizes from pooled interest. What prohibitions are involved?","expectedStructure":["Nature of the scheme","Riba element","Maisir element","FSC view","Conclusion"],"keyConcepts":["Riba","Maisir","Qimar","FSC"],"essentialPoints":["Prizes are precalculated interest","A few gain at others’ cost by chance","Diverts resources from the real sector"],"commonMistakes":["Thinking principal protection makes it lawful"],"topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61,64]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-1","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the prohibition of Riba and how it differs from a legitimate trade profit.","expectedStructure":["Define Riba","State the Qur'anic basis briefly as the book presents it (2:275-276)","Contrast with Bai' (trade) profit using Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman","Conclude with the underlying rationale (risk-sharing vs a guaranteed return)"],"keyConcepts":["Riba","Bai'","Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman"],"essentialPoints":["Riba is a guaranteed increment on a loan of money, unrelated to any real exchange of goods or services.","Trade profit is justified by the trader's service and by the business risk he bears (damage, storage, market/price risk).","Riba-based transactions violate Al-Kharaj bi-al-Daman: profit can only be claimed by a party willing to bear liability.","The Qur'an mentions the permissibility of trading immediately before prohibiting Riba, signaling trade as its intended alternative."],"commonMistakes":["Treating 'profit' and 'interest' as interchangeable just because both are a 'return on capital'.","Forgetting to explain the risk-bearing distinction, which is the actual basis for the different rulings.","Omitting that a trader may legitimately charge a higher credit price than the cash price, as long as it is fixed once and for all."],"topicId":"t3.2.1","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.1","pages":[44]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-2","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"What is Gharar, and why does its presence invalidate a contract in Islamic commercial law?","expectedStructure":["Define Gharar (excessive uncertainty/ambiguity)","Explain why the Shari'ah prohibits it (risk of dispute, exploitation, unjust enrichment)","Give the kind of subject matter/price uncertainty that triggers it","Note how contract-validity rules (certainty of price, subject matter, delivery) exist to avoid it"],"keyConcepts":["Gharar","Certainty of subject matter","Certainty of price"],"essentialPoints":["Gharar refers to excessive uncertainty or ambiguity about the existence, description, or deliverability of the subject matter or price of a contract.","It is prohibited because it can lead to dispute, exploitation of one party's ignorance, or unjust enrichment.","Rules such as requiring a certain price stipulated once and for all, and specific identification of the subject matter, exist specifically to guard against Gharar."],"commonMistakes":["Confusing Gharar (uncertainty) with Maisir (gambling), which are related but distinct prohibitions.","Assuming any risk at all is prohibited — normal business risk borne by a trader is not the same as contractual Gharar."],"topicId":"t3.2.2","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.2","pages":[57]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-3","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the prohibition of Maisir/Qimar (games of chance) and its relevance to modern financial products.","expectedStructure":["Define Maisir/Qimar","Explain the underlying harm (unearned gain at another's expense, dependence on pure chance)","Note its relevance to contracts/products the book flags elsewhere (e.g. conventional insurance)"],"keyConcepts":["Maisir","Qimar","Gharar"],"essentialPoints":["Maisir/Qimar refers to games of chance where gain to one party comes purely at the expense of another, determined by chance rather than productive effort or risk-sharing.","It is prohibited because it transfers wealth without any corresponding economic activity or service.","The book links this prohibition to its later critique of conventional insurance, where premium-for-uncertain-payout resembles a wager."],"commonMistakes":["Treating Maisir as identical to ordinary business risk-taking, which the Shari'ah permits.","Failing to connect this prohibition to why Takaful was developed as an alternative to conventional insurance."],"topicId":"t3.2.3","chapter":3,"source":{"chapter":3,"section":"3.2.3","pages":[61]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
